window.AIBRIEFS=window.AIBRIEFS||{};Object.assign(window.AIBRIEFS,{"1960/14":{"facts":"Act 10 of Arkansas's 1958 Second Extraordinary Session required every teacher in a state-supported school or college, as a condition of employment, to file an annual affidavit listing every organization to which the teacher had belonged or regularly paid dues or contributions in the preceding five years. Arkansas teachers had no tenure: they were hired year to year and could be let go at the end of any school year without charges, notice or a hearing. B. T. Shelton, who had taught in Little Rock for twenty-five years and was a member of the NAACP, refused to file the affidavit and his contract was not renewed; Max Carr, an associate professor at the University of Arkansas, and Ernest T. Gephardt, a Little Rock high school teacher, also refused and were told they could not be re-employed.","procedure":"Shelton, with the Arkansas Teachers Association, sued in federal court, where a three-judge District Court upheld Act 10 (174 F. Supp. 351); that decision came to the Supreme Court on appeal (No. 14). Carr and Gephardt sued in state court, and the Supreme Court of Arkansas also upheld the statute (231 Ark. 641); the Court granted certiorari (No. 83) and decided the two cases together.","question":"May a State, consistently with the Due Process Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment, require every public-school and state-college teacher to disclose every organization with which the teacher has been associated over the past five years, as a condition of employment?","holding":"No. Act 10 deprives teachers of their right of associational freedom protected by the Fourteenth Amendment because its unlimited and indiscriminate sweep goes far beyond what the State's legitimate interest in the fitness and competence of its teachers could justify. The judgments of both lower courts were reversed.","reasoning":"Justice Stewart began from two premises: a State plainly may investigate the competence and fitness of the teachers it hires, so the case differs from NAACP v. Alabama and Bates v. Little Rock, where the disclosure demanded bore no relevant relation to a state interest; but compelling a teacher to disclose every associational tie impairs the right of free association, a right closely allied to free speech. That impairment was aggravated because the teachers served at the absolute will of the boards receiving the affidavits, the statute did not require the information to be kept confidential, and the record showed that fear of public exposure and pressure was not groundless. The decisive point was breadth: the statute reached a teacher's church, political party and every social, professional or avocational tie, many of which could have no bearing on fitness to teach. Relying on cases such as Lovell v. Griffin, Schneider v. State, Cantwell v. Connecticut and Talley v. California, the Court held that even a legitimate and substantial governmental purpose \"cannot be pursued by means that broadly stifle fundamental personal liberties when the end can be more narrowly achieved,\" and that the breadth of a legislative abridgment must be viewed in light of less drastic means for achieving the same purpose. Measured that way, \"The unlimited and indiscriminate sweep of the statute now before us brings it within the ban of our prior cases.\"","separate":"Justice Frankfurter dissented, joined in his opinion by Justices Clark, Harlan and Whittaker. He wrote that he disliked the legislation but that it did not exceed the range of permissible state action: the careful selection of teachers depends on a comprehensive range of information, Arkansas could reasonably find the information germane, and if the affidavits were ever used to dismiss teachers solely for membership in unpopular organizations, that application could be struck down then. Justice Harlan also dissented, joined by Justices Frankfurter, Clark and Whittaker. He stressed that the cases presented no issue of racial discrimination on this record, that rights of association are not absolute, and that a requirement of this kind could not be said to exceed a State's conceded authority to determine the qualifications of its teachers.","disposition":"Reversed, 5 to 4. Opinion of the Court by Justice Stewart; Justices Frankfurter and Harlan each wrote a dissent, each joined by the other dissenters (Justices Frankfurter, Clark, Harlan and Whittaker).","model":"Claude Opus 5.5","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep364/usrep364479/usrep364479.pdf"},"1962/84":{"facts":"In 1942 Noia and two codefendants, Caminito and Bonino, were convicted in a New York court of felony murder and sentenced to life imprisonment. The only evidence against each was his signed confession. Caminito and Bonino appealed and eventually won release because their confessions were found to have been coerced in violation of the Fourteenth Amendment. Noia did not appeal, and the State later conceded that his confession was also coerced. He testified that he did not appeal partly because of the cost to his family, and his trial lawyer said he feared a retrial might bring a death sentence.","procedure":"After Noia's codefendants were freed, he sought coram nobis relief in the New York courts, which was denied because he had not appealed. The federal district court then denied habeas corpus, holding that his failure to appeal was a failure to exhaust state remedies under 28 U.S.C. § 2254. The Second Circuit reversed and ordered his release unless he was given a new trial, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"May a federal court grant habeas corpus relief to a state prisoner who has a conceded constitutional claim but who forfeited state review by failing to appeal in time, so that the state courts refused to reach the merits?","holding":"Yes. A state procedural default does not strip federal courts of power to grant habeas relief, and the adequate-and-independent-state-ground rule that limits Supreme Court direct review does not apply to habeas. Section 2254 covers only state remedies still open when the petition is filed. A federal judge may deny relief to a petitioner who deliberately bypassed state procedures, but only on a knowing and intelligent waiver standard, which Noia did not meet.","reasoning":"Justice Brennan traced habeas corpus from English common law and Bushell's Case to show that it has long been the remedy for any imprisonment contrary to fundamental law, including by court order. He read the Act of 1867 as extending federal habeas to its constitutional limit so that state prisoners would have a federal forum for constitutional claims. The exhaustion rule is a matter of comity that governs when the power is exercised, not whether it exists, and past decisions did not treat state-court decisions or defaults as ousting federal jurisdiction. The adequate-state-ground rule reflects limits on appellate review, while habeas lies against detention itself, and the only substantive law at issue is federal. A State's interest in enforcing its procedures is met by a limited discretion to deny relief to one who deliberately bypassed them, judged by the Johnson v. Zerbst standard of intentional relinquishment of a known right. Noia's choice, made under a real risk of the death penalty, was not such a bypass. The Court also overruled Darr v. Burford to the extent it required a petition for certiorari before habeas, and added that “Our decision today swings open no prison gates.”","separate":"Justice Clark dissented, joining Justice Harlan's opinion and adding that the ruling would bring a flood of mostly frivolous petitions, make state judgments subject to federal attack decades later, and upset federalism; he suggested legislation might be needed. Justice Harlan, joined by Justices Clark and Stewart, dissented: he argued that the decision breaks sharply with a century of precedent, that the adequate-and-independent-state-ground rule has constitutional roots and limits habeas just as it limits direct review, that the majority's waiver test is unworkable, and that New York's rule requiring a timely appeal was a reasonable ground the federal courts should respect.","disposition":"The judgment of the Court of Appeals was affirmed on different grounds. Justice Brennan wrote the opinion of the Court; Justices Clark, Harlan and Stewart dissented.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep372/usrep372391/usrep372391.pdf"},"1964/52":{"facts":"The Southern Conference Educational Fund, a civil rights group working for Black citizens in the South, its executive director Dombrowski, its treasurer Smith, and its attorney Waltzer were arrested by Louisiana officers under the state's Subversive Activities and Communist Control Law and Communist Propaganda Control Law. Their offices were raided and files seized. A state judge quashed the arrest warrants and suppressed the seized evidence, but officials kept threatening prosecution. The plaintiffs alleged the threats were not meant to win convictions but to harass them and discourage civil rights work, and that the statutes were overbroad.","procedure":"The plaintiffs sued in federal court under 42 U.S.C. section 1983 for an injunction and declaratory relief. A three-judge district court dismissed the complaint, finding no irreparable injury and holding that it should abstain until state courts could narrow the statutes; the individual plaintiffs were later indicted. The Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction.","question":"May a federal court enjoin threatened state criminal prosecutions under allegedly overbroad statutes that regulate expression, and is abstention appropriate while state courts have a chance to narrow them?","holding":"Yes. A prosecution under an overbroad statute regulating expression, brought or threatened to chill protected activity, can cause irreparable injury justifying a federal injunction, and abstention is inappropriate where a statute is attacked on its face as vague or overbroad or is applied to discourage protected activities. The challenged provisions were void on their face and could not be applied until a narrowing construction was obtained.","reasoning":"Justice Brennan acknowledged that federalism ordinarily bars federal interference with good-faith state prosecutions, since the mere possibility of an erroneous initial state ruling is not irreparable injury. But here defending a prosecution would not adequately protect First Amendment rights. Overbroad laws chill protected expression, and the “chilling effect” can come from the fact of prosecution regardless of its prospects, so that those affected should not have to risk prosecution to test their rights. The Court also relied on the allegations of arrests, seizures and threats as showing bad-faith harassment, which a state court's construction of the statute could not cure. Abstention would subject people to piecemeal criminal prosecutions that are poor vehicles for defining a vague statute, and no readily apparent narrowing construction was available. The definition of a subversive organization was as vague and broad as the one struck down in Baggett v. Bullitt, and the registration offense relied on a presumption from congressional citation of an organization as a Communist front, without the procedural safeguards required by Anti-Fascist Committee v. McGrath. The State must seek a narrowing construction in a noncriminal proceeding before it may ask to modify the injunction.","separate":"Justice Harlan, joined by Justice Clark, dissented: the decision effectively abolishes abstention in vagueness challenges to state criminal statutes involving the First Amendment, paralyzes state criminal processes, assumes without a showing that state courts will not protect federal rights, strikes the statutes without giving state courts a chance to narrow them, and lets plaintiffs avoid prosecution simply by reaching federal court first. He would have had the district court retain jurisdiction while abstaining.","disposition":"The judgment of the district court was reversed and the case remanded for a decree restraining the pending prosecutions, returning the seized papers, and barring enforcement of the provisions held void on their face. Justice Brennan wrote the opinion of the Court; Justices Black and Stewart took no part, and Justices Harlan and Clark dissented.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep380/usrep380479/usrep380479.pdf"},"1959/64":{"facts":"The Attorney General sued on behalf of the United States under the Civil Rights Act of 1957 against members of the Board of Registrars of Terrell County, Georgia. The complaint charged that the officials, in administering their offices, used various devices to discriminate on racial grounds against Black citizens who wished to register to vote, and sought an injunction.","procedure":"The district court dismissed the complaint, holding the enforcement provision unconstitutional because its language could reach purely private action, which it thought went beyond the Fifteenth Amendment, even though the complaint alleged only official action. The government appealed directly to the Supreme Court.","question":"May a federal court hold a statute unconstitutional in all applications because it might be read to reach private conduct beyond Congress's power, when the case before it involves only conduct that is clearly within that power?","holding":"No. A person to whom a statute may constitutionally be applied cannot challenge it on the ground that it might be unconstitutional as applied to others not before the court. Because the statute is clearly valid as applied to racial discrimination in voter registration by state officials, the complaint should not have been dismissed.","reasoning":"Justice Brennan said the power to declare acts of Congress unconstitutional rests on the duty to decide actual cases, and that courts should not anticipate constitutional questions or formulate broader rules than the facts require. The rule against challenging a law based on hypothetical applications to others is a rule of practice, with weighty reasons: courts should not consider every conceivable application of complex legislation, and a limiting construction can be given when a doubtful application actually arises. Exceptions exist, for example where a nonparty's rights would be impaired with no way to protect them, where the rule would chill speech, or in rare cases where a statute cannot be enforced in part, but none applied here. United States v. Reese was not followed to the extent it took a different approach. As applied, discrimination by state officials in the course of their duties is state action clearly within the Fifteenth Amendment, and the lack of exhausted state remedies does not change that because Congress may correct violations by every state official. Barney v. City of New York had been worn away by later decisions, and Congress may authorize the United States to sue to protect the public interest in observance of constitutional rights.","separate":"Justice Frankfurter, joined by Justice Harlan, concurred in the judgment: acts of Congress are presumptively valid, courts should apply a statute so as to save it, the measure is sustained under familiar principles, and it was unnecessary to canvass the many other opinions touching on these issues.","disposition":"The judgment of the district court was reversed. Justice Brennan wrote the opinion of the Court; Justices Frankfurter and Harlan concurred in the judgment.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep362/usrep362017/usrep362017.pdf"},"1968/413":{"facts":"Pearce was convicted in North Carolina of assault with intent to commit rape and sentenced to 12 to 15 years. Years later his conviction was reversed because an involuntary confession had been admitted, and on retrial he received eight years, which added to time already served exceeded his original sentence. Rice pleaded guilty in Alabama to four burglaries and received 10 years in total; after his convictions were set aside for lack of counsel, he was retried on three charges and sentenced to 25 years with no credit for the time he had served. Neither State offered any justification for the longer sentences.","procedure":"Each man sought federal habeas corpus. The district courts held the longer sentences unconstitutional, and the Fourth and Fifth Circuits affirmed. The Supreme Court granted certiorari in both cases.","question":"To what extent does the Constitution limit imposing a harsher sentence after a defendant has won a new trial and been convicted again, including whether time already served must be credited?","holding":"The double jeopardy guarantee against multiple punishments requires that punishment already served be fully credited against any new sentence for the same offense. Neither double jeopardy nor equal protection absolutely bars a more severe sentence on reconviction, but due process forbids vindictiveness, so reasons for a harsher sentence must appear affirmatively in the record and rest on objective information about the defendant's conduct after the original sentencing.","reasoning":"Justice Stewart relied on Ex parte Lange to hold that a person cannot be twice punished for the same offense, so time served must be subtracted from the new sentence. On the length of the sentence, the Court relied on United States v. Ball and Stroud: a defendant who has his conviction set aside may be retried, and the corollary is that any legally authorized sentence may be imposed, on the premise that the original conviction was nullified at his own request. Equal protection is not violated because a retrial yields a wholly new sentence after a wholly new trial and cannot sensibly be analyzed as an invidious classification. A judge may consider new information about the defendant's life and conduct since the first trial. But due process forbids penalizing a defendant for successfully attacking his conviction, and the fear of such retaliation could deter appeals. Because actual vindictiveness is hard to prove, the Court required that reasons for an increased sentence affirmatively appear, be based on identifiable post-sentencing conduct, and be recorded for appellate review. Neither State offered any justification, so the judgments were affirmed.","separate":"Justice Douglas, joined by Justice Marshall, concurred: double jeopardy forbids any second sentence exceeding the first, since a defendant should not have to run the gantlet of risk twice, and the interest in remaining free of double punishment outweighs the State's interest. Justice Black concurred in part and dissented in part: he agreed on credit and on rejecting the double jeopardy and equal protection claims, and would uphold Rice's result on the district court's finding of actual retaliation, but would reverse as to Pearce because the Court had no power to prescribe detailed procedural rules not found in the Constitution. Justice Harlan concurred in part and dissented in part: after Benton v. Maryland and Green v. United States, double jeopardy bars a greater punishment on retrial, though Pearce should be released only after his first sentence expires. Justice White joined the opinion except that he would allow an increased sentence based on any objective, identifiable data unknown to the judge at the first sentencing.","disposition":"The judgments of the Courts of Appeals in Nos. 413 and 418 were affirmed. Justice Stewart wrote the opinion of the Court.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep395/usrep395711/usrep395711.pdf"},"1964/34":{"facts":"Executive Order No. 8979 (1941) set aside land in Alaska as the Kenai National Moose Range, and Public Land Order No. 487 (1948) temporarily withdrew nearby lands from settlement, location, sale or entry. Neither expressly mentioned oil and gas leasing, and the Secretary of the Interior consistently treated the lands as open to leasing, issuing leases and approving development at great expense. The Griffin lessees applied for leases between 1954 and 1955 and received them in 1958; respondents applied in August 1958 and sought the same lands, arguing the lands had been closed to leasing until a 1958 regulation.","procedure":"The Department rejected respondents' applications because the land had already been leased to earlier applicants, and the Secretary affirmed. Respondents sued in the nature of mandamus. The district court granted summary judgment for the Secretary, but the D.C. Circuit reversed, holding that the orders had withdrawn the lands from leasing so that the earlier applications and leases were nullities. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did Executive Order No. 8979 and Public Land Order No. 487 bar the Secretary of the Interior from issuing oil and gas leases on the Moose Range lands, or must a court defer to his consistent interpretation that they did not?","holding":"The orders did not bar leasing. The Secretary's long-standing interpretation that they did not is reasonable, and courts must respect an agency's reasonable interpretation of its own orders and regulations, especially where it has been publicly consistent and relied upon.","reasoning":"Chief Justice Warren noted that the Secretary had consistently read both orders as not barring leases, that this construction was a matter of public record, and that almost the entire area had been developed at great cost in reliance on it, while respondents claimed no detrimental reliance on a contrary reading. Courts show great deference to the interpretation of those charged with administering a statute, and even more so for an administrative regulation, whose administrative interpretation controls unless plainly erroneous or inconsistent with it. The Court found the language reasonably bears the Secretary's reading: settlement, location, sale and entry all concern transfer of title, so “or other disposition” could be read to cover only title-conveying dispositions, a lease does not convey title, and the orders' separate mention of leasing statutes suggests leasing was not included. The later regulations and the Secretary's discretion to refuse leases also supported his reading. The Court distinguished Wilbur as turning on the history of the Pickett Act. The fish trap exception cut slightly the other way but was a carelessly placed reassurance, not enough to make the interpretation unreasonable, and the reading of Public Land Order No. 487 followed a fortiori.","disposition":"The judgment of the Court of Appeals was reversed. Chief Justice Warren wrote the opinion of the Court; Justices Douglas and Harlan took no part.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep380/usrep380001/usrep380001.pdf"},"1960/97":{"facts":"Rachel Brawner was a short-order cook for a private concessionaire running a cafeteria at the Naval Gun Factory in Washington, a restricted installation where classified weapons were developed. Her employer's contract with the Gun Factory barred employing anyone who failed its security requirements as determined by the Security Officer. The Security Officer found she did not meet them, she had to turn in her badge, and the Superintendent approved. A request for a hearing was denied as serving no useful purpose, and no specific grounds were given to her. Her employer offered her work at another restaurant, which she declined as inconvenient.","procedure":"Brawner and her union sued the Secretary of Defense and Gun Factory officers in federal district court to compel return of her badge. The district court granted summary judgment for the defendants, and the en banc Court of Appeals affirmed over four dissents. The Supreme Court granted certiorari because of an alleged conflict with Greene v. McElroy.","question":"Was the commanding officer authorized to deny Brawner access to the Gun Factory as he did, and did excluding her without notice of the grounds or a hearing deprive her of rights under the Due Process Clause of the Fifth Amendment?","holding":"Yes to authority and no to the due process claim. The Navy Regulations explicitly empowered the commanding officer to exclude her, and the Fifth Amendment did not require notice of specific grounds and a hearing in these circumstances.","reasoning":"Following Greene v. McElroy, the Court looked for explicit authority and found it in Article 0734 of the Navy Regulations, approved by the President, which let commanding officers admit tradesmen or their agents only as authorized, together with the long history of commanding officers' unquestioned power to exclude civilians from their commands. On due process, Justice Stewart stated that “The very nature of due process negates any concept of inflexible procedures universally applicable to every imaginable situation,” and that the required procedure depends on the nature of the government function and the private interest affected. Brawner's interest was not the right to follow a trade, since she remained free to work as a cook elsewhere; she lost only the opportunity to work at one military installation. The government was acting as proprietor managing the internal operation of a military establishment, like its dispatch of its own internal affairs, where it has traditionally had broad control, and like government employment that can be ended at will absent legislation. The Court assumed she could not be excluded for patently arbitrary or discriminatory reasons such as her religion or party, but here the stated reason was rational and consistent with the contract, and no badge of disloyalty or infamy was affixed that would foreclose other employment.","separate":"Justice Brennan, joined by the Chief Justice and Justices Black and Douglas, dissented. He doubted statutory authority but focused on due process: Brawner had a protected interest, and holding that the government's bare assertion of a valid reason ends inquiry nullifies the right not to be arbitrarily injured, since officials could use security requirements as a pretext for discrimination. She was given no process at all, and being labeled a security risk carries a stigma, so it was fundamentally unfair to remove her so summarily.","disposition":"The judgment of the Court of Appeals was affirmed. Justice Stewart wrote the opinion of the Court; Justices Brennan, Black and Douglas and the Chief Justice dissented.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep367/usrep367886/usrep367886.pdf"},"1959/41":{"facts":"In 1957 Little Rock and North Little Rock, Arkansas, amended their occupational license tax ordinances to require any organization operating in the city to give the city clerk, on request, its officers, purposes, dues and contributions paid and by whom, and other information, all of which would be public. The petitioners were custodians of records for the local NAACP branches. They supplied everything except the names of members and contributors, citing the anti-NAACP climate and fear of harassment, economic reprisal, and bodily harm. The record contained uncontroverted evidence that publicly identified members had faced threats and harassment and that members had withdrawn or declined to join.","procedure":"Each petitioner was tried, convicted, and fined for violating her city's ordinance. The Arkansas Supreme Court consolidated the cases and, with two justices dissenting, upheld the convictions, calling disclosure a mere incident to a permissible legal result. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Can a city, in enforcing an occupational license tax, constitutionally compel a local NAACP branch to disclose its membership lists, consistent with the freedom of association protected against the States by the Fourteenth Amendment?","holding":"No. On this record compulsory disclosure of the membership lists would significantly interfere with members' freedom of association, and the cities showed no sufficiently compelling interest to justify it. The convictions could not stand because the petitioners could not be punished for refusing to give information the cities could not constitutionally demand.","reasoning":"Freedom of association for advancing ideas and airing grievances is protected from state invasion by the Fourteenth Amendment, including against subtle interference, and compelled disclosure of affiliation can restrain it. The record showed that exposure of members had been followed by harassment and threats, and had deterred membership, so the threat to liberty was neither speculative nor remote. Where state action significantly encroaches on personal liberty, the State must show a compelling subordinating interest. The power to tax is fundamental, but a preamble's assertion does not establish a reasonable relation between the ordinance and that purpose. The Court found no relevant correlation between the licensing power and public disclosure of membership: the NAACP's described activities were not shown to fall within any taxed occupation, no tax claim had been made against the branches, and no exemption had been sought.","separate":"Justices Black and Douglas concurred in the judgment and substantially in the opinion. They would rest on the ground that the ordinances as applied violate First Amendment freedoms of speech and assembly, which are beyond abridgment by harassment, humiliation, or exposure by government.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Stewart delivered the opinion of the Court; Justices Black and Douglas concurred.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep361/usrep361516/usrep361516.pdf"},"1964/202":{"facts":"Griffin was tried for first-degree murder in California. He did not testify at the guilt phase, though he testified at the separate penalty phase. Under Article I, section 13 of the California Constitution, the trial court told the jury it could take his failure to explain or deny evidence within his knowledge as tending to indicate the truth of that evidence. The prosecutor also stressed in argument that the defendant knew what happened and had not taken the stand to deny or explain it.","procedure":"The jury convicted and imposed the death penalty, and the California Supreme Court affirmed before Malloy v. Hogan was decided. The Supreme Court granted certiorari to decide whether comment on a defendant's failure to testify violates the Fifth Amendment as applied to the States.","question":"Does the Fifth Amendment, applied to the States through the Fourteenth, forbid a prosecutor's comment, or a judge's instruction, that a defendant's silence at trial is evidence of guilt?","holding":"Yes. The Fifth Amendment, directly in federal court and through the Fourteenth Amendment in state court, forbids comment by the prosecution on the accused's silence and instructions by the court that such silence is evidence of guilt.","reasoning":"The Court noted that such comment would be error in a federal trial under Wilson v. United States, and that although Wilson rested on a statute, the question was whether the comment rule violates the Fifth Amendment itself. It treated the comment and the court's acquiescence as the equivalent of offering and admitting evidence from the accused's silence. Comment on refusal to testify is a remnant of the “inquisitorial system of criminal justice,” and it penalizes the exercise of a constitutional privilege by making its assertion costly. The Court rejected the argument that the inference is natural and irresistible, distinguishing what a jury may infer unaided from what it may infer when the court solemnizes silence into evidence, and noting that a defendant may stay silent for other reasons, such as fear of impeachment with prior convictions. Malloy v. Hogan requires the same standards for silence in federal and state proceedings.","separate":"Justice Harlan concurred, reluctantly, because Malloy applied the Fifth Amendment to the States in all its refinements. He objected to the incorporation doctrine and said that, but for Malloy, he would not apply this non-fundamental rule to the States. Justice Stewart, joined by Justice White, dissented: he found no compulsion to testify in comment on a defendant's choice not to, saw California's cautionary instruction as a way of articulating an inference the jury would draw anyway, and viewed procedural rules of this kind as a matter for state policy.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Douglas delivered the opinion of the Court; the Chief Justice took no part. The Court reserved whether an accused may require an instruction that silence be disregarded.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep380/usrep380609/usrep380609.pdf"},"1966/79":{"facts":"In 1961, fifteen white and Black Episcopal clergymen on a prayer pilgrimage to promote racial integration planned to use the segregated waiting room and restaurant at the Jackson, Mississippi, bus terminal, and expected to be arrested. Police officers ordered them to move on, they refused, and they were arrested under a Mississippi breach-of-peace statute. All witnesses agreed the ministers entered peacefully. The police said a threatening crowd of 25 to 30 followed them, while the ministers said no crowd was present. A municipal police justice, Spencer, convicted them and imposed the maximum sentence.","procedure":"One minister received a trial de novo in county court and won a directed verdict, and the other cases were dropped. The ministers then sued the officers and the judge in federal court under 42 U.S.C. section 1983 and for common-law false arrest. A jury found for the defendants. The Fifth Circuit held the judge immune, held that the officers had a good-faith defense at common law but not under section 1983 (reading Monroe v. Pape), and remanded for a new trial on the section 1983 claim because of prejudicial cross-examination, adding that the ministers could not recover if they had gone to Mississippi expecting an illegal arrest.","question":"Does section 1983 abolish the common-law immunity of judges for judicial acts, and may police officers defend a section 1983 false-arrest claim by showing good faith and probable cause when they acted under a statute later held unconstitutional? Do plaintiffs forfeit recovery by deliberately testing their rights while expecting arrest?","holding":"A judge remains immune from section 1983 damages for acts within his judicial role, because Congress did not abolish that common-law immunity. Police officers may assert the defense of good faith and probable cause in a section 1983 action, as at common law, but the case required a new trial because the evidence on that defense was disputed. The ministers did not consent to their arrest by peacefully exercising their right to use the waiting room while expecting to be arrested.","reasoning":"Judicial immunity was long established at common law and rests on the public interest in judges deciding cases independently and without fear of suits by disappointed litigants, even when malice is alleged. The legislative record gives no clear sign that Congress meant to abolish all common-law immunities, and the Court had so held for legislators in Tenney v. Brandhove. The common law gave officers no absolute immunity, but did not hold an officer liable for arresting on probable cause, and the same reasoning supports excusing an officer who reasonably believed a statute valid. Monroe v. Pape did not decide any immunity question and read section 1983 against the background of tort law, which includes this defense. An officer need not predict later constitutional rulings, but the officers here claimed they acted to prevent violence, which turned on disputed facts, and the verdict was tainted by irrelevant cross-examination. The ministers had a right to use the waiting room and did not trick or goad the officers.","separate":"Justice Douglas dissented. He read “every person” in section 1983 to include judges, pointed to the 1871 debates in which opponents warned that judges would be liable, and argued that absolute immunity is not needed to protect honest judges. He would exempt honest mistakes but not a judge who knowingly and intentionally deprives a person of civil rights, such as one who conspires with police.","disposition":"Affirmed in part, reversed in part, and remanded. Chief Justice Warren delivered the opinion of the Court; Justice Douglas dissented.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep386/usrep386547/usrep386547.pdf"},"1962/53":{"facts":"Los Angeles County narcotics officers watched a marijuana dealer, Murphy, meet George Ker in a car near an oil field, a meeting that repeated a sale Murphy had made to an undercover sergeant the night before, though the officers could not see anything pass between the men. After Ker eluded them with a U-turn, they traced his license plate to an address and learned from another officer that a previously reliable informant had said Ker bought marijuana from Murphy and sold it from his apartment. The officers went to the apartment, got a passkey from the building manager, and entered quietly without announcing themselves, fearing destruction of evidence. They saw a brick of marijuana on the kitchen sink, arrested George and Diane Ker, and then found more marijuana elsewhere in the apartment without a warrant.","procedure":"The Kers were convicted of marijuana possession and the California District Court of Appeal affirmed, finding probable cause, a lawful entry, and a valid search incident to arrest. The California Supreme Court denied review, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari as the first suitable case to apply Mapp v. Ohio.","question":"By what standard are state searches and seizures judged after Mapp v. Ohio, and under that standard was evidence seized after the officers entered the apartment unannounced and arrested the occupants admissible?","holding":"The Fourth Amendment's standard of unreasonableness applies to the States through the Fourteenth Amendment, and the same exclusionary sanction applies. Under that standard, a plurality found that the arrests rested on probable cause, that the unannounced entry was reasonable given the risk that evidence would be destroyed, and that the search incident to arrest was valid, so the convictions were affirmed.","reasoning":"Part I, for the Court, held that Mapp enforces the Fourth Amendment's own reasonableness standard against the States, but does not require rigid uniformity of working rules or disturb the primacy of trial courts' findings on reasonableness, which this Court may review independently on the record. In Parts II to V, a four-Justice plurality found probable cause for George Ker's arrest from the repeated meeting with Murphy together with the informant's corroborated tip, and for Diane Ker's arrest from the marijuana in plain view and the information that the apartment was a base for dealing. Entry without notice under California's judicial exception for exigent circumstances was not unreasonable, because the evidence could be destroyed quickly and Ker's evasive driving suggested he might expect the police. The search was incident to a lawful arrest, time was of the essence, and the brick in the kitchen was seen in plain view and was not a search. The plurality declined to reach the later search of Diane Ker's car because it was not properly raised.","separate":"Justice Harlan concurred in the result, rejecting Part I's holding that state searches are judged by the Fourth Amendment's own standards and preferring Fourteenth Amendment fundamental fairness, and he found no violation under that test. Justice Brennan, joined by the Chief Justice and Justices Douglas and Goldberg, joined Part I but dissented on the application: he would hold that the unannounced entry violated the Fourth Amendment, since the occupants were unaware of the officers and no activity suggested destruction of evidence, and that police experience with other suspects cannot supply the necessary showing.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Clark delivered the opinion of the Court as to Part I and announced the judgment, with Justices Black, Stewart, and White joining Parts II to V. Justice Harlan concurred in the result, and four Justices dissented.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep374/usrep374023/usrep374023.pdf"},"1959/126":{"facts":"State officers in Oregon, acting on information that petitioners had obscene films, obtained a warrant and searched petitioner Clark's home. They found no films but seized tape and wire recordings and a recording machine believed to have been used in wiretapping. Two Oregon state courts held the search warrant and the search unlawful and ordered the items suppressed, and the state indictment was dismissed. Federal officers then obtained the articles from a bank safe-deposit box under a federal warrant, and petitioners were indicted for intercepting and divulging telephone communications and conspiracy.","procedure":"The federal district court in Oregon assumed without deciding that the state search was unreasonable but denied the motion to suppress because no federal agent had known of or taken part in it. The articles were admitted and petitioners were convicted. The Ninth Circuit affirmed on the same ground, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"May articles obtained through an unreasonable search and seizure by state officers, without involvement of federal officers, be introduced over timely objection against a defendant in a federal criminal trial (the so-called silver platter doctrine)?","holding":"No. Evidence obtained by state officers in a search that would have violated the Fourth Amendment if conducted by federal officers is inadmissible over timely objection in a federal criminal trial, even without federal participation. The federal court must make its own inquiry into the reasonableness of the state search, whatever a state court has decided.","reasoning":"The Weeks rule that state-seized evidence was admissible rested on the premise that unreasonable state searches did not violate the Federal Constitution, and Wolf v. Colorado removed that premise in 1949 by holding that arbitrary police intrusion on privacy is enforceable against the States through the Fourteenth Amendment. Logic offers no basis for treating evidence taken in violation of the Fourth Amendment differently from evidence taken in violation of the Fourteenth. The Court exercised its supervisory power over federal evidence rules and weighed the exclusionary rule, whose purpose is to deter by removing the incentive to disregard the Constitution. Experience in the federal courts and in many states showed the rule had not crippled law enforcement. The old doctrine had also produced difficult case-by-case inquiries into federal participation and undercut state exclusionary policies, whereas exclusion would encourage honest federal-state cooperation. Finally, judicial integrity weighed against federal courts becoming accomplices in disobedience of the Constitution.","separate":"Justice Frankfurter dissented, arguing that the Fourth Amendment and the Fourteenth Amendment's Due Process Clause are not the same thing, doubting that the movement among the States toward exclusion was inexorable, and proposing instead that federal courts exclude such evidence where a state court enforcing its own rules had found the search unlawful and suppressed it. Justice Harlan, joined by Justices Clark and Whittaker, filed a memorandum stating that he would retain the nonexclusionary rule of Weeks and Byars, and otherwise joined Justice Frankfurter's dissent.","disposition":"Judgment of the Court of Appeals vacated and the case remanded to the district court for further proceedings, by a 5-4 vote, in an opinion by Justice Stewart.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep364/usrep364206/usrep364206.pdf"},"1959/59":{"facts":"Thompson, a long-time local resident, was in a Louisville cafe that sold food and beer, about half an hour on a Saturday evening, dancing by himself, when two officers came in on a routine check. The manager told an officer that Thompson had been there a little over half an hour and had bought nothing; Thompson said he was waiting for a bus and was arrested for loitering. Outside, the officer said Thompson was argumentative, and he was charged with disorderly conduct. The manager testified Thompson was a frequent patron who had never been told he was unwelcome, and other evidence showed he had money, a bus schedule, and a home address.","procedure":"The Louisville Police Court convicted Thompson of loitering and disorderly conduct and fined him $10 on each charge after denying his motions to dismiss for lack of evidence. Fines under $20 were not appealable in Kentucky courts, and the Kentucky Court of Appeals granted a stay so he could seek review. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does a conviction that rests on no evidence of guilt violate the Due Process Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment?","holding":"Yes. The loitering and disorderly conduct convictions were totally devoid of evidentiary support, and convicting and punishing a person without evidence of guilt denies due process.","reasoning":"The Court said the question turned not on the sufficiency of the evidence but on whether the conviction rested on any evidence at all, and the city agreed that a conviction without record support would be void. Under the loitering ordinance the only possible basis was inability to give a satisfactory account of oneself while loafing on premises without the owner's consent; the evidence showed no such facts, since Thompson said he was waiting for a bus, and the manager's undisputed testimony showed implied consent. The city conceded that no law made it an offense to dance or pat one's feet in a cafe. For disorderly conduct, the only evidence was the officer's statement that Thompson was argumentative after arrest, with nothing showing disruptive conduct, and the Court assumed that merely arguing with a police officer is not disorderly conduct under Kentucky law. It likened the case to convicting on a charge not made, which is a denial of due process.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded to the Police Court of Louisville for further proceedings. Justice Black delivered the opinion of the Court; the text does not state a vote or any separate opinions.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep362/usrep362199/usrep362199.pdf"},"1963/402":{"facts":"Borak, who owned 2,000 shares of J. I. Case Company stock acquired before the merger, challenged Case's merger with American Tractor Corporation. He alleged that the merger was approved by a small margin through a false and misleading proxy statement, which among other things failed to disclose alleged market manipulation of American Tractor stock, and that the merger would not have been approved otherwise. He sought to void the merger and recover damages for himself and similarly situated stockholders.","procedure":"The complaint had a diversity count for breach of fiduciary duty and a count under Section 14(a) of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934. The district court held that for the Section 14(a) count it could grant only declaratory relief, applied a Wisconsin security-for-expenses statute to the rest, set a $75,000 bond, and dismissed when the bond was not posted. The Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit reversed on both counts, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari limited to whether Section 27 authorizes a federal cause of action for rescission or damages.","question":"Does Section 27 of the Securities Exchange Act authorize a private federal action, including for rescission or damages, by a stockholder for a violation of Section 14(a) in a proxy solicitation for a merger?","holding":"Yes. Private parties may sue under Section 27 for violations of Section 14(a), in both derivative and direct actions, and federal courts may grant remedies beyond declaratory relief. The particular remedy awaits trial on the merits.","reasoning":"Section 27 gives district courts jurisdiction over suits to enforce any liability or duty created by the Act, and Section 14(a) aims at deceptive proxy solicitation, so its purpose of protecting investors implies judicial relief. Because injury from a deceptive proxy usually flows from harm to the corporation, excluding derivative suits would amount to denying private relief. Private enforcement supplements the Commission, which examines over 2,000 proxy statements a year and cannot independently check each. Federal courts must provide the remedies needed to make effective the congressional purpose, citing Bell v. Hood, Deckert, and Lincoln Mills. The presence of state law questions does not change the federal character of the right, and limiting federal courts to declaratory relief would push victims into state courts that might attach no liability.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Clark delivered the opinion of the Court; the text does not state a vote or any separate opinions.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep377/usrep377426/usrep377426.pdf"},"1968/12":{"facts":"Desist and others were convicted of conspiring to import and conceal heroin. An important part of the Government's evidence was tape recordings of conversations among several petitioners in a New York City hotel room, made by federal agents in the adjoining room using a microphone taped to the door on their own side, with no physical intrusion into the petitioners' room. The eavesdropping occurred before December 18, 1967, the date of Katz v. United States, and was not authorized by a warrant. Two admitted trespassory surveillance episodes were also disclosed, but the district judge found, after a hearing, that neither tainted the trial evidence.","procedure":"The district court and the Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit rejected the Fourth Amendment objection because there was no trespass or actual intrusion into a protected area, and the convictions were affirmed. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Should the rule of Katz v. United States, that electronic eavesdropping without a warrant violates the Fourth Amendment even absent physical intrusion, be applied to electronic surveillance conducted before Katz was decided, including in cases still on direct review?","holding":"No. To the extent Katz departed from earlier decisions, it applies only where the prosecution seeks to introduce the fruits of electronic surveillance conducted after December 18, 1967, so the earlier eavesdropping here did not require reversal.","reasoning":"Katz was a clear break with the past even if it was foreshadowed, because earlier cases kept applying the trespass test and had not formally overruled Olmstead and Goldman. Under Linkletter and Stovall, retroactivity turns on the purpose of the new rule, law enforcement reliance on the old rule, and the effect on the administration of justice. The purpose of extending the exclusionary rule is deterrence of police misconduct, which retroactive application would not advance because the conduct has already occurred. Officers and courts had justifiably relied on the trespass cases, including a recent Second Circuit decision on a nearly identical installation, and litigating whether past eavesdropping tainted evidence would burden courts. The same reasons undercut any distinction for cases pending on direct review, because deterrence and reliance look to the time of the search, and Mapp differs since the States already knew they were bound by the Fourth Amendment. The Court also rejected the petitioners' other challenges, agreeing with the lower courts that the disclosed surveillance had not tainted the evidence.","separate":"Justice Black concurred in the judgment for the reasons in his Katz dissent, while adhering to his dissent in Linkletter. Justice Douglas dissented, objecting that giving Katz himself the new rule but denying it to Desist is not even-handed justice, so the decision was not truly wholly prospective. Justice Harlan dissented (Douglas joined Part I), arguing that all new constitutional rules must at least apply to cases pending on direct review, that the Court should vacate and remand in light of Katz, and discussing separately how retroactivity should work on habeas corpus. Justice Fortas dissented (also in Kaiser v. New York), arguing that Olmstead had long been undermined by Silverman, Clinton, and Berger, so Katz was not a clear break, and that denying a fundamental constitutional protection to those searched before Katz was unjustified and rewarded police foot-dragging.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Stewart delivered the opinion of the Court; Justice Marshall took no part, and Justices Black (concurring), Douglas, Harlan and Fortas (dissenting) filed separate opinions.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep394/usrep394244/usrep394244.pdf"},"1962/83":{"facts":"The Philadelphia National Bank (PNB) and Girard Trust Corn Exchange Bank were the second and third largest of the 42 commercial banks headquartered in the four-county Philadelphia metropolitan area, with branches throughout it. In November 1960 their boards approved a consolidation under the PNB charter. The resulting bank would have had roughly 36 percent of the area banks' assets and deposits, and the two largest banks would have held about 59 percent of assets. The Comptroller of the Currency approved the merger even though reports from the Federal Reserve Board, the FDIC and the Attorney General all said it would substantially lessen competition.","procedure":"The United States sued in the Eastern District of Pennsylvania to enjoin the merger under § 1 of the Sherman Act and § 7 of the Clayton Act. The District Court ruled for the banks, holding among other things that § 7 did not apply to bank mergers, that the relevant market was broader than the four counties, and that competition was not likely to be substantially lessened. The United States appealed directly to the Supreme Court.","question":"Does § 7 of the Clayton Act, as amended in 1950, apply to bank mergers despite the Bank Merger Act of 1960, and if so, does the proposed PNB-Girard merger violate it?","holding":"Section 7 of the Clayton Act as amended reaches bank mergers, and the Bank Merger Act of 1960 neither repealed nor immunized bank mergers from it. A merger that produces a firm with an undue share of the relevant market and significantly increases concentration is presumed likely to lessen competition substantially, and this merger (at least 30 percent share, a 33 percent rise in two-firm concentration) violates § 7.","reasoning":"Congress in 1950 meant to close a loophole by covering the whole range of corporate amalgamations from pure stock to pure asset acquisitions, so mergers fall under § 7 even though the asset provision is limited to corporations subject to the FTC; any other reading would leave a loophole in a statute designed to close one, and immunity from the antitrust laws is not lightly implied. The Bank Merger Act did not impliedly repeal § 7: it gives no express immunity, the Comptroller need not weigh competition in any particular way, and the committee reports said the antitrust laws were unaffected, so primary jurisdiction does not apply either. The relevant product market is commercial banking and the relevant geographic market is the four-county metropolitan area, where small and mid-sized customers can practically bank. Given the market share and concentration figures, the Court adopted a presumption of illegality that the banks' evidence did not rebut, treating the lay testimony of vigorous competition and available alternatives as of little weight. The banks' justifications (following customers to the suburbs, competing with large out-of-state banks for large loans, and stimulating local economic development) were rejected because de novo branching is available, countervailing power cannot justify anticompetitive effects in one market, and weighing social and economic benefits was a choice Congress had already made. Banking's regulation makes competition more, not less, important: “competition is our fundamental national economic policy.”","separate":"Justice Harlan, joined by Justice Stewart, dissented. He argued that bank mergers are a distinct field, that for ten years everyone including the Justice Department assumed the 1950 amendment did not cover them, and that Congress in the Bank Merger Act deliberately chose a regulatory public-interest approach rather than § 7, which the Court now largely nullifies. He also read the 1950 amendment and its history as aimed at industrial concentration and the asset-acquisition loophole, not bank consolidations. Justice Goldberg, in a separate memorandum, joined Harlan on the Clayton Act question but did not necessarily dissent from the judgment, since there was a substantial Sherman Act issue he did not reach. Justice White took no part.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded with direction to enter judgment enjoining the merger. Justice Brennan delivered the opinion of the Court; Justice White took no part, and Justices Harlan, Stewart and Goldberg dissented in part as described.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep374/usrep374321/usrep374321.pdf"},"1965/762":{"facts":"Cassidy and Johnson were arrested in New Jersey in January 1958 for felony murder and interrogated by police, each receiving only a warning that his statements must be voluntary and could be used against him. Each gave a recorded confession that was admitted at their joint trial after the judge found it voluntary; petitioners offered no rebuttal and their lawyers told the jury the confessions were true. They were convicted and sentenced to death, and their convictions became final about six years before this decision. In a later collateral proceeding they filed affidavits claiming they had asked for a lawyer or to contact relatives and been refused, which the state court accepted as true for the Escobedo issue.","procedure":"After repeated unsuccessful state and federal attacks on voluntariness, petitioners sought post-conviction relief in New Jersey, arguing the confessions were inadmissible under Escobedo v. Illinois. The New Jersey Supreme Court held that Escobedo did not affect convictions already final when it was decided. The Supreme Court granted certiorari and also considered whether Miranda v. Arizona, decided a week earlier, should be retroactive.","question":"Should Escobedo v. Illinois and Miranda v. Arizona be applied retroactively to defendants whose trials were completed, or whose convictions were final, before those decisions?","holding":"Escobedo applies only to trials that began after June 22, 1964, and Miranda only to trials that began after June 13, 1966. Neither is retroactive, including to cases tried earlier that were still on direct appeal.","reasoning":"Under Linkletter v. Walker and Tehan v. Shott, the Court may make a new criminal procedure rule prospective, looking to the rule's purpose, reliance on prior decisions, and the effect on the administration of justice. The choice does not depend on the importance of the constitutional right, and the Court gave retroactive effect only where a rule goes to the integrity of fact-finding, as with Gideon and Jackson v. Denno. Escobedo and Miranda mainly protect the privilege against self-incrimination and guard against unreliable statements, but in situations where the danger is less than with overt coercion. Defendants already tried can still invoke the voluntariness case law, which takes account of failure to warn or allow outside assistance. Law enforcement fairly relied on earlier cases such as Crooker and Cicenia, and retroactivity would force retrial or release of many prisoners convicted on trustworthy evidence. The same reasons support applying the rules only to trials begun after the decisions, giving officers fair notice. Petitioners' voluntariness claim was barred by an adequate state procedural ground, and their other claims lacked merit.","separate":"Justice Clark concurred in the opinion and judgment, adhering to his separate opinion in Miranda. Justices Harlan, Stewart and White concurred in the opinion and judgment but continued to believe, for reasons in their Miranda dissents, that the new rules were unjustified and unwise. Justice Black, joined by Justice Douglas, dissented, saying petitioners were entitled to the protections of the Fifth and Sixth Amendments as construed in Escobedo and Miranda, for reasons given in his dissent in Linkletter v. Walker.","disposition":"Affirmed. The opinion of the Court was by Chief Justice Warren, announced by Justice Brennan; Justices Black and Douglas dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep384/usrep384719/usrep384719.pdf"},"1959/69":{"facts":"Federal narcotics officers executing a search warrant found narcotics and paraphernalia in a bird's nest in an awning outside a window of a Washington, D.C. apartment where Jones was present, and arrested him. Jones testified that the apartment belonged to a friend, Evans, who had given him use of it and a key, that he had slept there perhaps a night, and that Evans was away in Philadelphia. The warrant rested solely on an officer's affidavit relaying an unnamed informant's report that Jones and another person sold and kept heroin in the apartment, that the informant had bought there the day before, that the informant had earlier given accurate information, that other sources gave the same information, and that Jones was known to use narcotics.","procedure":"Jones moved before trial to suppress the evidence for lack of probable cause, and the District Court denied the motion solely because he lacked standing. He was convicted on two narcotics counts and sentenced to seven years. The Court of Appeals, one judge dissenting, affirmed, agreeing he lacked standing but also holding the evidence lawful, and it rejected a new claim that the warrant was executed in violation of 18 U.S.C. § 3109. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Could Jones, who was present in a friend's apartment with permission and was charged with possessing the seized narcotics, challenge the search as a 'person aggrieved' under Rule 41(e), and was the search warrant supported by probable cause based on hearsay?","holding":"Jones had standing under Rule 41(e): possession was the basis of the charge, and he also had a sufficient interest as someone legitimately on the premises. The hearsay affidavit, supported by a substantial basis for crediting it, established probable cause. The challenge to execution of the warrant under § 3109 could not be resolved on the record and was remanded.","reasoning":"A 'person aggrieved' must be a victim of the search, but requiring a defendant charged with possession to claim ownership or possession of the property or premises puts him in a dilemma, since his allegations could help convict him or tempt him to perjury. The Government cannot prosecute for possession while arguing that the defendant had no possession at the time of the search, so possession alone gives standing. The Court also declined to import fine property-law distinctions among lessees, licensees, invitees and guests, holding that anyone legitimately on the premises where a search occurs may challenge it when its fruits are used against him, as Jones's testimony showed. On probable cause, following Draper v. United States, hearsay may support a warrant if there is a substantial basis for crediting it. Here the informant had given accurate information, other sources corroborated it, and Jones was a known user. Because the § 3109 claim turned on conflicting trial testimony about how the warrant was executed, the Court vacated and remanded for the District Court to consider it.","separate":"Justice Douglas joined the part of the opinion on standing but dissented from the holding that there was probable cause. He argued that the magistrate's duty is nondelegable, that the magistrate must know the evidence on which police propose to act and make an independent judgment, and that reliance on faceless informers makes the magistrate a tool of police interests, referring to his dissent in Draper.","disposition":"Judgment of the Court of Appeals vacated and the case remanded to the District Court. Justice Frankfurter delivered the opinion of the Court; Justice Douglas dissented in part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep362/usrep362257/usrep362257.pdf"},"1963/138":{"facts":"Petitioners were subpoenaed to testify before the bistate Waterfront Commission of New York Harbor about a work stoppage at the Hoboken, New Jersey piers. After they refused to answer on self-incrimination grounds, they were granted immunity from prosecution under the laws of New Jersey and New York. They still refused, saying their answers might incriminate them under federal law, to which the state immunity did not extend.","procedure":"Petitioners were held in civil and criminal contempt. The New Jersey Supreme Court reversed the criminal contempt conviction on procedural grounds but affirmed the civil contempt judgments, relying on United States v. Murdock, Feldman v. United States and Knapp v. Schweitzer for the rule that a State may compel testimony that could be used in a federal prosecution. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"May one jurisdiction in the federal system, having immunized a witness from prosecution under its own laws, compel him to give testimony that might incriminate him under the laws of another jurisdiction?","holding":"The privilege against self-incrimination protects a state witness against incrimination under federal as well as state law, and a federal witness against incrimination under state law. A state witness may be compelled to testify under a state grant of immunity only because the Federal Government is barred from making any use of the compelled testimony and its fruits. Feldman was overruled.","reasoning":"Decided the same day as Malloy v. Hogan, which made the privilege fully applicable to the States, the case required reconsidering the older rule. The privilege reflects many values, including sparing suspects the trilemma of self-accusation, perjury or contempt, and most are defeated when a witness can be whipsawed into incriminating himself under both state and federal law. The Court reviewed English and early American cases, finding that Saline Bank and Ballmann v. Fagin supported protection, that the English rule after McRae protected witnesses against foreign-law incrimination, and that the contrary dictum in Hale v. Henkel and the reasoning in Murdock rested on a misreading of English authority. Feldman's premise, drawn from the silver platter doctrine for illegally seized evidence, had been discarded in Elkins v. United States, and Knapp rested on the rule rejected in Malloy. Applying Counselman v. Hitchcock, a witness may be compelled only if the testimony and its fruits cannot be used by officials of the other government, so the Court prohibited federal use, with the federal authorities bearing the burden of showing an independent source if the witness shows he testified to related matters. This lets States obtain needed information and leaves the witness and the Federal Government as if the privilege had been claimed. Petitioners could therefore now be required to answer, though the contempt judgments were vacated because they had reasonably relied on Feldman.","separate":"Justice Black concurred in the judgment and opinion, adding his earlier dissents in Feldman, Bartkus and similar cases. Justice Harlan, joined by Justice Clark, concurred in the judgment only: he thought Feldman's constitutional holding correct, found the Court's historical analysis inadequate and said it improperly blended the Fifth and Fourteenth Amendments, but would bar federal use of state-compelled testimony under the Court's supervisory power, following Elkins. Justice White, joined by Justice Stewart, concurred, saying the protection against federal use, whether supervisory or constitutional, suffices, and warning against any broader rule requiring absolute immunity from prosecution, which would invalidate state immunity statutes and impair state law enforcement.","disposition":"Judgment of the New Jersey Supreme Court affirmed in part (petitioners may be ordered to answer), the contempt judgment vacated, and the case remanded. Justice Goldberg delivered the opinion of the Court.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep378/usrep378052/usrep378052.pdf"},"1965/52":{"facts":"In 1961 Shott was tried in an Ohio court for violating the Ohio Securities Act; he did not testify, and the prosecutor commented extensively on that silence, as Ohio's constitution permitted. His conviction was affirmed and all direct review ended in 1963, before the Court decided Malloy v. Hogan (1964), which applied the Fifth Amendment privilege against self-incrimination to the States, and Griffin v. California (1965), which held that adverse comment on a defendant's failure to testify violates the privilege.","procedure":"Shott sought federal habeas corpus. The district court dismissed his petition, but the Sixth Circuit reversed, reasoning from Malloy that the privilege includes freedom from comment on a refusal to testify. The Supreme Court granted certiorari and asked the parties to address whether Griffin applies retroactively.","question":"Does the rule of Griffin v. California, forbidding adverse comment on a defendant's failure to testify, apply retroactively to a state conviction that became final before Griffin was decided?","holding":"No. The Court was not able to say that the Griffin rule requires retrospective application, so it does not apply to cases whose direct review was exhausted before Griffin was decided.","reasoning":"Following Linkletter v. Walker, the Constitution neither prohibits nor requires retroactive effect, and the Court weighs the rule's purpose, the reliance placed on the prior rule, and the effect on the administration of justice. Griffin's purpose lies in the values of the privilege itself, which concern the integrity of an accusatorial system rather than protecting the innocent from conviction or the reliability of the guilty verdict, unlike rules on counsel or coerced confessions. For more than fifty years, from Twining through Adamson and later cases, the Court had held the privilege did not bind the States and never reached the comment question, and Ohio and California amended their constitutions to permit comment, so the States relied in good faith. Retroactivity would affect about six States in which comment had probably occurred in every trial where the defendant did not testify, and retrials of old cases would often be impossible.","separate":"Justice Black, joined by Justice Douglas, dissented for the reasons stated in his Linkletter v. Walker dissent. Chief Justice Warren and Justice Fortas took no part.","disposition":"Judgment vacated and case remanded to the Sixth Circuit to consider Shott's remaining habeas claims. Justice Stewart wrote the opinion of the Court.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep382/usrep382406/usrep382406.pdf"},"1967/29":{"facts":"New York Penal Law § 781-b made it a crime to distribute in quantity handbills concerning candidates in an election without printing the name and address of the printer and of the person at whose instance they were distributed. Zwickler was convicted of distributing anonymous handbills criticizing a Congressman seeking re-election in 1964. His conviction was reversed on state-law grounds, because the People had not shown distribution \"in quantity,\" and the state's highest court affirmed. He alleged that he wished to keep distributing anonymous handbills in future elections and feared prosecution.","procedure":"Zwickler sued in the Eastern District of New York under the Civil Rights Act and the Declaratory Judgment Act, seeking a declaration that the statute was facially overbroad under the First Amendment and an injunction against future prosecutions. A three-judge court, one judge dissenting, abstained and dismissed, leaving him to raise the claim in a state prosecution or a state declaratory action, reasoning that he had shown no special circumstances warranting an injunction. The Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction.","question":"Did the federal district court err in abstaining from a declaratory judgment on the facial validity of a state statute challenged as overbroad under the First Amendment, because the plaintiff had not shown grounds for enjoining state prosecutions?","holding":"Yes. Abstention was not warranted because the statute was challenged for overbreadth and was not susceptible to a state-court construction that would avoid the constitutional question. The district court had a duty to decide the request for a declaratory judgment regardless of whether an injunction was proper.","reasoning":"Since Reconstruction Congress has given federal courts jurisdiction over constitutional claims, which imposes a duty \"to give due respect to a suitor’s choice of a federal forum for the hearing and decision of his federal constitutional claims.\" Abstention is allowed only in narrowly limited special circumstances, such as a state statute open to a construction that would avoid or modify the federal question. Here Zwickler attacked the statute as overbroad, not vague, and the State conceded that no state construction could narrow it, so abstention would only give state courts the first chance at the federal claim, which McNeese and Turner forbid. The principle matters especially for First Amendment claims, because delay from state proceedings could itself chill protected expression. Dombrowski v. Pfister shows that abstention and injunctive relief are separate questions, so the lower court's view on injunctions did not decide whether to hear the declaratory claim. Douglas v. City of Jeannette concerned only an injunction. On remand the district court must decide whether the prerequisites for a declaratory judgment are met and whether an injunction is appropriate; the Court expressed no view on the merits.","separate":"Justice Harlan concurred in the judgment. He agreed that abstention was inappropriate here and that injunctive relief was not pertinent at this stage, but he did not accept that the key question for abstention is whether a complaint alleges overbreadth rather than vagueness, since the distinction is unclear and would make abstention a pleader's option. He also found the chilling-effect doctrine unhelpful here and would examine each case in light of the interests abstention serves.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded. Justice Brennan delivered the opinion of the Court.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep389/usrep389241/usrep389241.pdf"},"1964/11":{"facts":"Florida Statutes § 798.05 made it a crime for a Black man and a white woman, or a white man and a Black woman, who were not married to each other, to habitually live in and occupy the same room at night, punishable by up to twelve months in prison or a $500 fine. Other provisions in the same chapter punished adultery, lewd cohabitation and fornication for all couples regardless of race, but those required proof of intercourse and § 798.05 did not. Appellants, an unmarried interracial couple, were charged under § 798.05, and the State offered evidence on each element: habitual nighttime occupation of a room, by a Black person and a white person, who were not married.","procedure":"The jury convicted appellants, and the trial judge overruled their equal protection objections. The Florida Supreme Court affirmed solely on the authority of Pace v. Alabama, and the Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction. It addressed only the equal protection issue.","question":"Does a state law that punishes an unmarried interracial couple for habitually occupying the same room at night, but does not punish same-race couples for the same conduct, deny equal protection of the laws?","holding":"Yes. Section 798.05 denies the equal protection of the laws guaranteed by the Fourteenth Amendment. Florida showed no overriding purpose requiring that this conduct be punished only when engaged in by a white person and a Black person, and the law was not shown to be necessary to enforce the State's ban on interracial marriage.","reasoning":"Pace v. Alabama had upheld a statute because it treated whites and Blacks within the class alike, but that narrow view did not survive later cases holding that classifications must be reasonable in light of their purpose, so equal application within the class is not enough. Racial classifications are \"constitutionally suspect\" and subject to the most rigid scrutiny, especially in a criminal statute. The State said the purpose was to prevent breaches of sexual decency, but nothing made it essential to punish promiscuity of an interracial couple and not that of other couples, and the general adultery, lewd cohabitation and fornication statutes already covered the field. The State also argued that the law was ancillary to its ban on interracial marriage. The Court declined to decide that ban's validity and held that even if it were valid, the cohabitation law must be examined independently. Such a law is upheld only if \"necessary, and not merely rationally related,\" to a permissible state policy, and Florida did not show that the neutral laws against illicit behavior could not serve its marriage policy as well.","separate":"Justice Harlan concurred, joining the opinion and adding that the necessity test, developed in First Amendment cases, applies equally to state racial discrimination, and that the statute fails whether it is viewed as ancillary to the marriage ban or as resting on an independent state interest. Justice Stewart, joined by Justice Douglas, concurred in the judgment but rejected any implication that an overriding purpose could justify a criminal law making the race of the actor the test of criminality, calling such discrimination \"invidious per se.\"","disposition":"Reversed. Justice White delivered the opinion of the Court.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep379/usrep379184/usrep379184.pdf"},"1963/461":{"facts":"Herbert Aptheker and Elizabeth Gurley Flynn, native-born citizens and leading officials of the Communist Party of the United States, held valid passports. In 1961 a final order of the Subversive Activities Control Board required the Party to register, and the Supreme Court had upheld that registration requirement. Section 6 of the Subversive Activities Control Act of 1950 then made it a crime, punishable by up to five years in prison and a $10,000 fine, for a member of a registered Communist organization, with knowledge or notice of the order, to apply for or use a passport. The State Department revoked appellants' passports, and they conceded that revocation was proper if § 6 was constitutional.","procedure":"After administrative hearings and review by the Board of Passport Appeals and the Secretary of State sustained the revocations, appellants sued in the District Court for the District of Columbia for a declaration that § 6 violated the Fifth Amendment and an order to issue passports. A three-judge court upheld § 6 as reasonably related to protecting national security and granted summary judgment for the Secretary. The Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction.","question":"Does § 6 of the Subversive Activities Control Act, which bars members of registered Communist organizations from obtaining or using passports, violate the liberty protected by the Fifth Amendment's Due Process Clause?","holding":"Yes. Section 6 is unconstitutional on its face because it sweeps too broadly and indiscriminately across the right to travel, which is part of the liberty guaranteed by the Fifth Amendment. The Court also declined to save the statute by upholding it as applied to these top-ranking Party leaders.","reasoning":"Under Kent v. Dulles the right to travel abroad is part of the liberty of which a citizen cannot be deprived without due process, and denying a passport effectively prohibits travel outside the Western Hemisphere. Congress may protect national security, but not by means that sweep unnecessarily broadly into protected freedoms when less drastic means exist. Section 6 applies to every member regardless of whether the member knows the organization's aims, is active in it or is committed to its purposes, and thus rests on an irrebuttable presumption that members will endanger security. It also ignores the purpose and destination of the travel, so a member could be criminally liable for visiting a sick relative or reading manuscripts in Oxford, while one traveling to Canada or Latin America for hostile ends is unaffected. Less drastic alternatives existed, such as the Federal Employee Loyalty Program treating membership as one factor, and the Executive Branch's own 1958 proposals did not make membership alone disqualifying. Judged by its plain import, the section fails because \"precision must be the touchstone of legislation so affecting basic freedoms.\" Narrowing it for appellants alone would require substantial rewriting and inject vagueness, and as with First Amendment freedoms, appellants need not show that Congress could not have written a valid statute reaching their travel.","separate":"Justice Black concurred in the holding but not on the ground that the Due Process Clause alone gives a liberty to travel abroad; he viewed the whole Act as invalid as a bill of attainder, as punishment without a jury trial, and as a violation of First Amendment freedoms. Justice Douglas concurred, adding that freedom of movement is kin to the rights of assembly and association, that being a Communist is not a crime, and that only illegal conduct, not travel itself, may be punished. Justice Clark, joined by Justice Harlan and, as to Part I, Justice White, dissented: the Court should have decided the statute only as applied to appellants, who as top Party leaders plainly knew of the Party's registration and aims, and the section is a reasonable regulation tailored to Congress's findings about the Communist threat.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded. Justice Goldberg delivered the opinion of the Court; Justice White joined only Part I of Justice Clark's dissent and would have affirmed.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep378/usrep378500/usrep378500.pdf"},"1963/548":{"facts":"Two Houston police officers applied to a justice of the peace for a warrant to search Aguilar's home for narcotics. Their affidavit said only that they had \"received reliable information from a credible person\" and believed that heroin, marijuana, barbiturates and other narcotics were being kept at the premises for sale and use. The warrant issued, and police announced themselves at the door, heard a commotion, forced their way in, and seized Aguilar as he tried to dispose of a packet of narcotics. The record did not show that any other information was given to the magistrate.","procedure":"Aguilar's objection to the evidence obtained under the warrant was overruled at trial, and he was convicted of illegal possession of heroin and sentenced to 20 years. The Texas Court of Criminal Appeals affirmed, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Is a search warrant supported by an affidavit that states only that the officers received reliable information from a credible, unnamed person that narcotics are on the premises consistent with the Fourth and Fourteenth Amendments?","holding":"No. The affidavit did not give the magistrate a sufficient basis to find probable cause, so the warrant should not have issued and the evidence was inadmissible. An affidavit may rest on hearsay, but the magistrate must be told some of the underlying circumstances from which the informant concluded the narcotics were there and from which the officer concluded the informant was credible or his information reliable.","reasoning":"Under Ker v. California the standard of reasonableness for obtaining a search warrant is the same under the Fourth and Fourteenth Amendments. Magistrates' determinations are preferred and get substantial deference, but only if the magistrate performs a neutral and detached function and does not act as a rubber stamp for the police. Under Nathanson and Giordenello, a mere affirmance of belief or suspicion is not enough, and the magistrate must judge for himself the persuasiveness of the facts relied on. Here the conclusion was not even the affiant's own but an unidentified informant's, and nothing showed that the informant spoke from personal knowledge. Hearsay is permissible, but the magistrate needs some of the underlying circumstances for both the informant's conclusion and the officer's judgment of credibility, as in the detailed affidavit sustained in Jones v. United States. Otherwise the inferences would be drawn by an unidentified informant or the police rather than by a neutral magistrate, and an officer could evade Nathanson by passing his suspicion to a colleague. Any surveillance not presented to the magistrate was irrelevant.","separate":"Justice Harlan concurred, saying he would have affirmed but for Ker v. California; given Ker, he could not relax the Giordenello standards, which he viewed as reflecting constitutional requirements. Justice Clark, joined by Justices Black and Stewart, dissented, arguing that Nathanson and Giordenello were distinguishable because this affidavit named a source of reliable information from a credible person, Giordenello rested on a federal rule rather than the Constitution, hearsay may support a warrant, and the officers also had a week of surveillance. He said the Court replaced the flexible probable cause standard with a rigid, technical formula.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded. Justice Goldberg delivered the opinion of the Court.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep378/usrep378108/usrep378108.pdf"},"1959/443":{"facts":"Warrior & Gulf Navigation Co. transports steel by barge and repairs its barges at a terminal in Chickasaw, Alabama, where its employees were represented by the Steelworkers. Between 1956 and 1958 the company reduced the unit from 42 to 23 workers, in part by contracting maintenance work previously done by its employees to other firms, which used the company's supervisors and hired some of the laid-off men at reduced wages. The union filed a grievance charging that the contracting out violated the agreement and amounted to a partial lockout. The agreement had no-strike and no-lockout clauses and a grievance procedure ending in an umpire's decision, but it excluded from arbitration \"matters which are strictly a function of management.\" The company refused to arbitrate.","procedure":"The union sued under § 301(a) of the Labor Management Relations Act to compel arbitration. The District Court dismissed after hearing evidence, much of it on the merits, holding that contracting out was strictly a function of management and excluded from arbitration. The Court of Appeals affirmed by a divided vote, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"In a suit under § 301 to compel arbitration, may a court deny arbitration of a grievance over contracting out by holding that it falls within a clause excluding matters \"strictly a function of management\" from arbitration?","holding":"No. Judicial inquiry under § 301 is limited to whether the party agreed to arbitrate the grievance, and an order to arbitrate should not be denied unless it can be said with positive assurance that the arbitration clause is not susceptible of an interpretation covering the dispute, with doubts resolved in favor of coverage. The contracting-out grievance was therefore for the arbitrator.","reasoning":"Arbitration of labor disputes is the substitute for industrial strife, not for litigation, so judicial hostility toward commercial arbitration has no place here. A collective bargaining agreement is a generalized code that creates a system of industrial self-government and a common law of the shop, and the grievance machinery gives meaning to it. The arbitrator draws on the practices of the industry and shop and on the parties' trust in his judgment, competence that courts lack. Arbitration remains a matter of contract, and courts decide whether the party agreed to arbitrate, but they must confine themselves to that question. Where an absolute no-strike clause exists, in effect everything management does is subject to the agreement. The exclusion for \"strictly a function of management\" refers only to matters over which the contract gives management complete control and unfettered discretion, otherwise the exception would swallow the arbitration clause. The agreement contained no express provision excluding contracting out, only the most forceful evidence of an intent to exclude could prevail given the vague exclusion and broad arbitration clause, and a grievance that contracting out violates the agreement is a dispute over its meaning and application.","separate":"Justice Frankfurter concurred in the result. The text notes that Justice Brennan wrote a separate opinion, joined by Justices Frankfurter and Harlan, printed in the companion case, which is not in the text reviewed here. Justice Whittaker dissented, arguing that arbitrators' power rests on the parties' clear and plain agreement and cannot be implied, that the Court's presumption of coverage is a new doctrine, and that the lower courts correctly found, based on 19 years of the parties' dealings and the union's failed bargaining attempts, that contracting out was excluded from arbitration. Justice Black took no part.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Douglas wrote the opinion of the Court, announced by Justice Brennan.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep363/usrep363574/usrep363574.pdf"},"1964/82":{"facts":"Article VI, section 2 of the Texas Constitution allowed a member of the armed forces to vote only in the county where he lived when he entered service, for as long as he remained in the service. Petitioner, an Army sergeant who entered service from Alabama in 1946, moved to El Paso in 1962, where he bought a house, ran a small business, paid property taxes, and registered his car. The State conceded that he was domiciled in Texas and meant to stay permanently, and that but for his uniform he would be eligible to vote in El Paso County. His military post was at White Sands, New Mexico, and he commuted from El Paso.","procedure":"Petitioner was refused the right to vote and sought a writ of mandamus. The Texas Supreme Court, with two Justices dissenting, upheld the provision and refused the writ, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does a state constitutional provision that permanently bars a member of the armed forces who moves to the State from voting there while in service, even if he is a bona fide resident, violate the Equal Protection Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment?","holding":"Yes. A State may impose reasonable residence requirements for voting, but it may not deny the ballot to a bona fide resident merely because he is a member of the armed forces.","reasoning":"The Court acknowledged Texas's broad power over voter qualifications and residence requirements, but held that treating all servicemen alike does not end the inquiry, because classifications must be reasonable in light of their purpose. Texas offered two justifications. The fear that concentrated military voting would let service members take over local civilian affairs was rejected, because bona fide residents cannot be fenced out of the franchise because of how they might vote. The argument that servicemen are transient was also rejected, because Texas offers students, hospital patients, and civilian federal employees a chance to prove bona fide residence, and its courts decide residence of servicemen in other contexts such as divorce. By making the presumption of nonresidence conclusive and incapable of rebuttal, the provision exceeded what is needed to protect the residence requirement, and States \"may not casually deprive a class of individuals of the vote because of some remote administrative benefit to the State.\" The Court emphasized that Texas remains free to take reasonable steps to ensure that applicants are actually bona fide residents.","separate":"Justice Harlan dissented. He argued that history and precedent show the Equal Protection Clause was not meant to reach state voter qualifications, as he had argued in his dissent in Reynolds v. Sims, and that the Court's authorities did not support its extension of federal judicial power. Even assuming the Clause applies, he thought Texas could rationally treat servicemen sent there by military order as presumptively transient, avoid the cost of case-by-case residence inquiries, and protect local politics from military voting strength in light of the tradition of civilian control of the military and Texas's own history.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Stewart wrote the opinion of the Court; Justice Harlan dissented, and the Chief Justice took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep380/usrep380089/usrep380089.pdf"},"1960/41":{"facts":"Six murders near Evansville, Indiana, in 1954 and 1955 were heavily covered by local media and aroused great excitement and indignation in Vanderburgh County and adjoining Gibson County. After Irvin's arrest, the prosecutor and police issued widely publicized press releases saying he had confessed to all six. He was indicted for one murder, obtained a change of venue to Gibson County, and was denied a second change despite alleging that publicity had prejudiced that county too. Of a 430-person jury panel, 268 were excused for cause as having fixed opinions on his guilt, and eight of the twelve jurors finally seated admitted they thought him guilty, though each said he could be impartial.","procedure":"Irvin was convicted of murder and sentenced to death, and the Indiana Supreme Court affirmed. After his claim was held unexhausted and the case remanded once by the Supreme Court, the Seventh Circuit decided the merits against him in his federal habeas corpus proceeding. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Was Irvin denied a fair trial by an impartial jury, in violation of the Fourteenth Amendment's Due Process Clause, given the pretrial publicity and the jurors' admitted opinions of his guilt?","holding":"Yes. In light of the build-up of prejudice in the community and the voir dire, in which most seated jurors admitted a belief in his guilt, the finding of impartiality did not meet constitutional standards, so his conviction and death sentence are void; the State may retry him within a reasonable time.","reasoning":"The Indiana single-change-of-venue statute is not invalid on its face because the Indiana Supreme Court reads it to allow a second change where necessary for an impartial jury. The Court said “A fair trial in a fair tribunal is a basic requirement of due process,” and a juror must decide only on the evidence, though jurors need not be wholly ignorant of the case; it is enough that they can lay aside an impression. Because whether a juror's opinion raises a presumption of partiality is a mixed question, the federal court had a duty to independently evaluate the voir dire. The record showed a barrage of newspaper, radio and television coverage, including reports of his confession and prior crimes, and a pattern of deep and bitter prejudice in the community. About 90 percent of those examined had some opinion of guilt, and two-thirds of the seated jurors had one. Given human frailty and the influence of an opinion once formed, the jurors' assurances of impartiality could be given little weight.","separate":"Justice Frankfurter concurred, agreeing with the opinion but adding that this was not an isolated case of trial by newspaper, that prejudicial publicity, often with the prosecutor's collaboration, repeatedly distorts jury trials, and that the Court had not decided that fair criminal justice must give way to press freedom.","disposition":"Judgments of the Court of Appeals and District Court vacated and the case remanded for the State to be allowed a reasonable time to retry Irvin. Justice Clark wrote the opinion of the Court; the text does not state the vote, and Justice Frankfurter concurred.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep366/usrep366717/usrep366717.pdf"},"1963/220":{"facts":"About 64 faculty, staff and students of the University of Washington challenged two Washington loyalty-oath statutes. The 1931 law required teachers to swear to support the federal and state constitutions and to promote, by precept and example, respect for the flag and institutions, reverence for law and order, and undivided allegiance to the United States government. The 1955 law, applied through University forms, required all state employees to swear they were not a “subversive person” or a member of the Communist Party or a subversive organization as defined in the state Subversive Activities Act, under penalty of perjury; the Act defined subversive persons and organizations by reference to acts or advocacy of overthrowing government by revolution, force or violence.","procedure":"The challengers brought a class action seeking a declaratory judgment and injunction. A three-judge District Court held the 1955 oath not unduly vague and not violative of the First and Fourteenth Amendments, and abstained from ruling on the 1931 oath pending state-court construction, dismissing the action. The Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction.","question":"Are the 1955 and 1931 Washington oath requirements unconstitutionally vague under the Due Process Clause, and should the federal court have abstained as to the 1931 oath pending state-court interpretation?","holding":"Both oaths and their underlying statutes are unduly vague, uncertain and broad and violate due process, especially because they inhibit First Amendment freedoms. Abstention was not appropriate as to the 1931 oath, since a state-court construction could not cure its vagueness.","reasoning":"Following Cramp v. Board of Public Instruction, laws that require or forbid conduct in terms so vague that people must guess at their meaning violate due process. The 1955 oath could be read to reach those who teach, advise, or aid members of the Communist Party, or support causes Communists support, and it was unclear what knowledge is required and whether it reaches peaceful but fundamental change (“revolution”). The 1931 oath's promise to promote respect for the flag and institutions and undivided allegiance has an indefinitely wide range, from flag-salute refusals to criticism of government policy. Because such vagueness abuts sensitive First Amendment areas, conscientious oath-takers must steer far wider of the unlawful zone, and the possibility of a perjury prosecution, however remote, cannot be dismissed because prosecutors are well-intentioned; as the Court put it, “It is not the penalty itself that is invalid but the exaction of obedience to a rule or standard that is so vague and indefinite as to be really no rule or standard at all.” Abstention is discretionary and requires special circumstances; here the oath is open to an indefinite number of constructions, an abstract construction would not remove the vagueness and might raise other constitutional issues, and delay would be costly to protected speech.","separate":"Justice Clark, joined by Justice Harlan, dissented. He argued that the 1955 Act is not vague, distinguishing Cramp's broader oath and relying on Gerende v. Board of Supervisors (identical Maryland language) and Dennis v. United States (the Smith Act), that appellants' other First Amendment and due process claims were frivolous in light of cases like Adler and Garner, and that Washington's courts should have had the chance to construe the 1931 oath, as the District Court held.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice White wrote the opinion of the Court; the opinion text states no vote count beyond the two dissenters, Justices Clark and Harlan.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep377/usrep377360/usrep377360.pdf"},"1963/7":{"facts":"Appellants were chiropractic graduates who wanted to practice in Louisiana without meeting the educational requirements of the Louisiana Medical Practice Act. In 1957 they sued the State Board of Medical Examiners in federal district court, seeking an injunction and a declaration that the Act, as applied to them, violated the Fourteenth Amendment. A three-judge court, on its own motion, invoked the abstention doctrine because a state-court ruling that chiropractors are not covered by the statute might end the controversy, and stayed the case while retaining jurisdiction. In the Louisiana courts appellants unreservedly briefed and argued both the state-law question and their Fourteenth Amendment claim.","procedure":"The Louisiana courts held that the Act applied to chiropractors and, so applied, did not violate the Fourteenth Amendment, and the Louisiana Supreme Court declined review. Appellants did not seek review in the Supreme Court but returned to the District Court, which dismissed because the state courts had decided all issues including the federal ones and the remedy was appeal to the Supreme Court. Appellants appealed directly to the Supreme Court.","question":"May a litigant who was sent to state court under the abstention doctrine, and who there presented his federal constitutional claims along with the state-law question, return to the federal district court for decision of the federal claims?","holding":"A litigant remitted to state court by an abstention order has a right to return to federal court for his federal claims, but he forfeits that right if he freely and without reservation submits those claims to the state courts for decision, litigates them, and has them decided there. Because appellants reasonably but mistakenly believed Government Employees v. Windsor required them to litigate their federal claims in state court, the rule was not applied against them and the case was remanded for the District Court to decide the merits.","reasoning":"Forcing a litigant who properly invoked federal jurisdiction to accept a state court's decision of his federal claims would conflict with Congress's grants of jurisdiction, and abstention is only the postponement of the exercise of federal jurisdiction, not its abdication. Review in the Supreme Court is no adequate substitute for a federal trial court's role in building the record and finding facts. NAACP v. Button recognized, however, that a party may elect to forgo the federal forum by seeking a complete and final state adjudication, and the Court held that a party who voluntarily and without reservation litigates his federal claims in the state courts has so elected, whether or not he seeks review in the Supreme Court. Windsor requires only that a litigant inform the state courts of his federal claims so the statute can be construed in light of them, not that he litigate them. A litigant can protect his right by reserving, on the state record, the right to return to the federal court, though an explicit reservation is not indispensable unless it clearly appears he voluntarily did more than Windsor required.","separate":"Justice Douglas concurred, arguing at length that the Pullman abstention doctrine should be reappraised: it causes delay and expense, was made mandatory rather than discretionary, and is especially harmful in civil rights cases; he thought the Court's new election rule would become a trap for the unwary and that any presumption should run the other way, and proposed alternatives such as discretionary referral, declaratory-judgment or certification procedures. Justice Black concurred in part and dissented in part, joining the judgment and opinion insofar as the District Court's stated reasons for dismissal were wrong, but he would have affirmed the dismissal because the complaint raised no substantial federal question.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded to the District Court for decision on the merits of the Fourteenth Amendment claims. Justice Brennan wrote the opinion of the Court; the text does not state a vote count, and Justices Douglas and Black wrote separately.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep375/usrep375411/usrep375411.pdf"},"1962/202":{"facts":"Sanders was arrested for robbing a federally insured bank. Appearing without counsel in federal district court in January 1959, he waived counsel and indictment, signed a waiver, pleaded guilty to an information, and was sentenced to 15 years; before sentence he mentioned his narcotics use and asked to go to a facility for addiction cure. In 1960 he filed a pro se motion under 28 U.S.C. section 2255 alleging only conclusions (an invalid indictment, denial of counsel, and coerced plea). Eight months later he filed a second motion alleging specific facts: that he had been mentally incompetent at trial and sentencing because jail medical authorities had administered narcotics to him as a known addict.","procedure":"The District Court denied the first motion without a hearing, saying it stated only conclusions and that the files refuted its charges, and Sanders did not appeal. It denied the second motion without a hearing because he should have raised mental incompetency in the first. The Ninth Circuit affirmed, holding that the District Court could in its discretion decline a second motion based on facts known when the first was filed. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"What standards govern whether a federal court must grant a hearing on a second or successive section 2255 motion by a federal prisoner, and did the District Court properly refuse a hearing on Sanders's second motion?","holding":"A prior denial may be given controlling weight only if the same ground was determined adversely on the prior application, that determination was on the merits, and the ends of justice would not be served by reaching the merits of the new application. If the ground is new or was not adjudicated on the merits, a hearing may be refused only for abuse of the remedy, which the Government must plead. Here the first motion was not decided on the merits, so Sanders was entitled to a hearing on the second.","reasoning":"At common law and in federal habeas corpus, a prior denial was not res judicata, because the Court said “Conventional notions of finality of litigation have no place where life or liberty is at stake and infringement of constitutional rights is alleged.” Section 2244 codified the Salinger v. Loisel principle without changing the law, and section 2255's “similar relief” clause must be read as its equivalent because section 2255 was meant to provide a remedy exactly commensurate with habeas corpus. A ground means a sufficient legal basis for relief, doubts about sameness go to the applicant, the prior ruling must have been on the merits (with a full hearing where facts are disputed), and the applicant may show that the ends of justice call for redetermination. Where a ground is new or not decided on the merits, as in Wong Doo and Price v. Johnston, it can be refused only for abuse, such as deliberately withholding a ground, and the Government bears the burden of pleading it. Here the first motion was denied for pleading deficiency, and the files could not conclusively refute the allegation of narcotic-induced incompetence, which rests on facts outside the record. The sentencing judge has discretion on remand about whether the prisoner must be produced, and should handle first motions liberally to avoid later successive ones.","separate":"Justice White concurred in the result without opinion. Justice Harlan, joined by Justice Clark, dissented. He said the case belongs with Townsend v. Sain and Fay v. Noia as a trilogy that undercuts finality, that sections 2244 and 2255 give the District Court discretion to deny a successive motion presenting a ground that was available earlier, that the Court had misread the word “new” in section 2244 and left the burden of proving abuse unclear, and that on this record, where the trial transcript showed Sanders understood the proceedings and the first motion made an inconsistent claim, the denial was a proper exercise of discretion; he would have affirmed and he doubted the wisdom of such guideline decisions.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded to the District Court for a hearing consistent with the opinion. Justice Brennan wrote the opinion of the Court; Justice White concurred in the result, and Justices Harlan and Clark dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep373/usrep373001/usrep373001.pdf"},"1967/85":{"facts":"A federal grand jury in Connecticut charged Jackson and two others with transporting from Connecticut to New Jersey a kidnaped person who had been held for ransom and was harmed when liberated, in violation of the Federal Kidnaping Act. The Act provided that offenders “shall be punished (1) by death if the kidnaped person has not been liberated unharmed, and if the verdict of the jury shall so recommend, or (2) by imprisonment for any term of years or for life.” The statute set out no procedure for imposing death on a defendant who pleaded guilty or waived a jury.","procedure":"The District Court dismissed the kidnaping count, holding the Act unconstitutional because it made “the risk of death” the price of asserting the right to jury trial. The Government appealed directly to the Supreme Court, which noted probable jurisdiction.","question":"Does a federal statute that allows the death penalty only upon a jury's recommendation unconstitutionally burden the rights to plead not guilty and to demand a jury trial, and if so, must the whole statute fall?","holding":"The death penalty clause impermissibly burdens the exercise of constitutional rights because it can be imposed only on defendants who assert the right to jury trial. The clause is severable, however, so the rest of the Act remains valid and the indictment should not have been dismissed.","reasoning":"The Court rejected the Government's readings of the Act: the word “shall” and the statute's history show that Congress meant a jury recommendation to bind the judge, and the Act does not authorize a specially convened penalty jury for defendants who plead guilty or waive a jury, a novel procedure courts should not create. Thus a defendant who forgoes a jury is assured of escaping execution while one who demands a jury risks death, which discourages assertion of Fifth and Sixth Amendment rights. The goal of limiting death sentences to cases where a jury recommends them is legitimate, but “Congress cannot impose such a penalty in a manner that needlessly penalizes the assertion of a constitutional right,” and alternatives such as jury sentencing in every case exist. The Court declined to cure the problem by instructing judges to reject all guilty pleas and jury waivers, since that must come from Congress. On severability, the clause is functionally independent, the 1932 Act had no death penalty, and Congress would clearly have enacted the kidnaping prohibition without it.","separate":"Justice White, joined by Justice Black, dissented. He would not hold the provision unconstitutional, because the Court itself says not every guilty plea or jury waiver is influenced by it; he would instead reverse and direct that pleas and waivers be carefully examined to ensure they are neither coerced nor encouraged by the death penalty power, and he objected to beginning to invalidate statutes on their face for arguably burdening the jury right.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded. Justice Stewart delivered the opinion of the Court; Justice Marshall took no part, and the text does not otherwise state the vote count (Justices White and Black dissented).","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep390/usrep390570/usrep390570.pdf"},"1960/40":{"facts":"Rogers was arrested in Connecticut in January 1954 with a revolver that ballistic tests tended to link to a fatal liquor-store shooting in West Haven. He was taken without court order from jail for questioning and interrogated for hours by police. When he kept denying the shooting, an Assistant Chief of Police pretended in his hearing to telephone officers to bring in his wife for questioning, and after about an hour Rogers said he would confess. He confessed that night and again the next day before the Coroner, after his lawyer was turned away from the jail. At a hearing without the jury, Rogers testified that he had asked for a lawyer and that the wife-threat was why he confessed; the police denied this.","procedure":"The Connecticut trial judge found the confessions voluntary and let the jury consider them, and the Connecticut Supreme Court of Errors affirmed the murder conviction. Rogers then sought federal habeas corpus; after one round of appeal and remand, the District Court denied the writ on the state court's findings and the Second Circuit affirmed over one dissent. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did the Connecticut courts, in admitting the confessions as voluntary partly because they were probably true, apply a standard consistent with the Due Process Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment, and if not, what follows for the conviction?","holding":"A confession's admissibility must be decided under a standard that asks only whether the police conduct overbore the defendant's will, with no regard to whether the confession was probably true or false. Because the Connecticut trial judge and Supreme Court of Errors took probable reliability into account, the conviction was constitutionally invalid and the State must be given a chance to retry Rogers.","reasoning":"The Court said involuntary confessions cannot support a conviction not because they are unreliable but because our system is accusatorial, so the State may not prove guilt out of the accused's own mouth through coercion. It read the trial judge's findings and jury charge, and the state supreme court's opinion, as asking whether the pressure induced an untrue statement, and concluded the correct inquiry was whether the officers' conduct overbore Rogers' will, \"a question to be answered with complete disregard of whether or not petitioner in fact spoke the truth.\" The Court refused to decide for itself, or to have a federal habeas court decide, whether the confessions were in fact coerced, because the state court's findings of historical fact might have been distorted by the wrong standard and the defendant is entitled to have the issue tried by a state judge or jury under proper standards. A federal de novo hearing would neither protect defendants adequately nor respect the States' role. The remedy is a remand so the State can retry Rogers within a reasonable time.","separate":"Justice Stewart, joined by Justice Clark, dissented. He accepted that the state courts gave some weight to the probable truth of the confessions, but argued that the state courts' failure to state the correct test was not itself a constitutional error. In his view, habeas corpus should issue only if a coerced confession was in fact admitted, so he would remand to the District Court for a plenary hearing on that question.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded to the Court of Appeals to be held so the State can retry petitioner within a reasonable time, failing which he is to be discharged. Justice Frankfurter delivered the opinion of the Court; the opinion text does not state a vote count, and Justices Stewart and Clark dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep365/usrep365534/usrep365534.pdf"},"1962/118":{"facts":"Rhode Island created a Commission to Encourage Morality in Youth, charged with educating the public about obscene or indecent publications and recommending prosecutions. The Commission reviewed paperbacks and magazines and sent a wholesale distributor, Silverstein, notices on official stationery declaring listed titles objectionable for sale or display to those under 18. The notices thanked him in advance for his cooperation, said lists went to police, and reminded him of the Commission's duty to recommend prosecution. Police visited him afterward to see what he had done. Silverstein stopped filling orders, pulled unsold copies from retailers, and returned them to the publishers rather than risk court action. The state's Attorney General conceded that some listed publications were not obscene.","procedure":"Four New York publishers sued in the Rhode Island Superior Court for a declaration and an injunction. That court upheld the statute on its face but enjoined the Commission's practices; the Rhode Island Supreme Court affirmed on the facial question but reversed the injunction. The publishers appealed to the Supreme Court.","question":"Does the Rhode Island Commission's system of notifying distributors that publications are objectionable, backed by threats of prosecution but with no formal sanctions, violate the First and Fourteenth Amendments?","holding":"The Commission's system of informal censorship violates the Fourteenth Amendment. It operates as a scheme of prior administrative restraint with no notice, hearing, or judicial review, and so lacks the procedural safeguards required when a State regulates obscenity.","reasoning":"The Court began from the principle that States may regulate obscenity only through procedures that protect constitutionally protected expression, which is often separated from obscenity by a dim and uncertain line. It rejected the argument that the Commission only advised distributors, finding that, though limited to informal sanctions, it deliberately set out to suppress listed publications and succeeded; the distributor's compliance was not voluntary given the thinly veiled threats and police follow-up. The Commission's acts under color of state law were state action. The Court held the practice operates as \"a system of prior administrative restraints,\" which bears a heavy presumption of invalidity and has been tolerated only under judicial superintendence. Here there is no judicial review, no notice or hearing for the publisher, a vague mandate, and complete suppression for adults as well as youths. The Court also held the publishers had standing, because the Commission's acts directly curtailed circulation of their books. It added that genuine consultation between law enforcement officers and distributors to aid compliance is not forbidden, but this was a scheme to suppress.","separate":"Justice Douglas concurred, adhering to his Roth views on the narrow scope of power to suppress obscenity and calling the system censorship that is incompatible with the First Amendment. Justice Black concurred in the result. Justice Clark concurred in the result, reading the Court's holding narrowly: the Commission may not issue 'orders' or make thinly veiled threats, but may publicize its findings, give them to distributors and law enforcement officials, and seek prosecutions. Justice Harlan dissented, arguing that the Court gave too little weight to the State's interest in combating juvenile delinquency and that the Commission's advisory conduct is not self-executing and leaves courts open to challenge; he would remit the publishers to challenges of particular publications and affirm.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded for further proceedings not inconsistent with the opinion. Justice Brennan delivered the opinion of the Court; the opinion text does not state a vote count. Justices Black and Clark concurred in the result and Justice Harlan dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep372/usrep372058/usrep372058.pdf"},"1960/66":{"facts":"Police officers suspected that a house in Washington, D.C. was the headquarters of a gambling operation. With the owner's permission they used the vacant adjoining row house as an observation post and, for at least three days in April 1958, listened with a \"spike mike\", a microphone with a spike about a foot long. They pushed the spike under a baseboard and into the party wall until it touched a heating duct serving petitioners' house, which turned the whole heating system into a conductor of sound. The officers heard conversations on both floors, including petitioners' share of telephone calls, and later testified about them at trial.","procedure":"Petitioners were convicted in the District Court for the District of Columbia on three counts of gambling offenses, with the officers' testimony admitted over timely objection. The Court of Appeals affirmed, holding that neither the Communications Act of 1934 nor the Fourth Amendment was violated, relying on Goldman and On Lee; Judge Washington dissented. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Was the officers' testimony about conversations overheard through a spike mike inserted into the party wall and touching the heating duct admissible, or did the eavesdropping violate the Communications Act or the Fourth Amendment?","holding":"The testimony should not have been admitted. The Court found no interception under section 605 of the Communications Act, but held that the eavesdropping was done by \"an unauthorized physical penetration into the premises occupied by the petitioners\", which violated their Fourth Amendment rights.","reasoning":"On the statute, the Court agreed with the Court of Appeals: as in Irvine and Goldman, the officers' apparatus was not connected to the telephone facilities, so they had not intercepted communications within section 605. On the Fourth Amendment, the Court declined to reconsider Goldman, On Lee, or Olmstead or to address newer electronic devices, because the facts here did not require it. It reasoned that those earlier cases stressed that there had been no physical encroachment on a constitutionally protected area, whereas here the officers overheard petitioners only by usurping part of their house, the heating system, without their knowledge or consent. The Court said it need not decide whether there was a technical trespass under local property law, because Fourth Amendment rights are not measured by ancient niceties of tort or real property law. The decision rested on the reality of an actual intrusion into a constitutionally protected area, and the Court said it would \"decline to go beyond it, by even a fraction of an inch\" the earlier decisions rather than reexamine Goldman.","separate":"Justice Douglas concurred, saying that cases like Goldman and this one turn on irrelevant differences in equipment and depth of penetration; in his view the wrong is the invasion of the home's privacy, which is a search requiring a warrant, and the trespass concept is beside the point. Justices Clark and Whittaker concurred, joining the Court's opinion because the majority found a physical penetration sufficient to take the case outside earlier decisions.","disposition":"Reversed; the convictions were set aside. Justice Stewart delivered the opinion of the Court; Justice Douglas filed a concurring opinion, and Justices Clark and Whittaker filed a joint concurrence. The text shows no dissent and states no vote count.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep365/usrep365505/usrep365505.pdf"},"1968/130":{"facts":"Respondent Family Finance Corp. brought a garnishment action in Wisconsin against petitioner, naming her employer as garnishee, on a $420 promissory note claim. The employer answered that it held $63.18 in her unpaid wages, would pay half to her as a subsistence allowance, and would hold the other half subject to the court's order. In Wisconsin the clerk issues the summons at the request of the creditor's lawyer, and serving the garnishee freezes the wages without prior notice or hearing for the debtor; the wages are released only if she later wins on the merits of the main suit. Here petitioner was served the same day as her employer.","procedure":"Petitioner moved to dismiss the garnishment as violating Fourteenth Amendment due process. The Wisconsin courts upheld the procedure, the Wisconsin Supreme Court sustaining the lower court, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does Wisconsin's prejudgment garnishment procedure, which freezes a wage earner's wages before judgment with no prior notice or opportunity to be heard, violate procedural due process under the Fourteenth Amendment?","holding":"Yes. Wisconsin's prejudgment wage garnishment procedure, which takes the use of the debtor's wages without notice and a prior hearing, violates the fundamental principles of procedural due process, at least where no special creditor or state interest is present.","reasoning":"The Court said the sole question was not whether the Wisconsin law was wise but whether property was taken without the procedural due process the Fourteenth Amendment requires, and framed it as whether \"the interim freezing of the wages without a chance to be heard\" violates that guarantee. Summary procedures might suffice in extraordinary situations, but here no special state or creditor interest was shown, the statute was not narrowly drawn to any such condition, and in personam jurisdiction over petitioner was easily obtained. Wages are a specialized type of property, and a procedure acceptable for attachments in general does not necessarily satisfy due process for all property in its modern forms. Prejudgment garnishment can impose tremendous hardship on wage earners with families, can lead to loss of a job, gives the creditor enormous leverage to extract collection fees, and, given Wisconsin's inadequate exemption, may drive a family to the wall. Where the taking is so obvious, the Court concluded, it needs no extended argument that, \"absent notice and a prior hearing\", the procedure offends due process.","separate":"Justice Harlan concurred, explaining that the property taken is the use of the garnished wages in the interim, which is not de minimis, so she must receive notice and a prior hearing aimed at establishing the validity or probable validity of the underlying claim; he viewed due process as limited by fundamental fairness drawn from Anglo-American legal heritage and not satisfied by simultaneous notice to the debtor or the eventual chance to win the main suit. Justice Black dissented, arguing that nothing in the Constitution authorizes the Court to strike down a state policy it considers unwise, that the decision rests on policy rhetoric, that petitioner showed no injury beyond a trivial sum and raised injuries of others, that the Wisconsin law gave notice and a hearing on the merits, and that the Court was overruling prior decisions such as McKay v. McInnes and reviving a natural-law view of due process.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Douglas delivered the opinion of the Court. The text does not state the vote count; Justice Harlan concurred and Justice Black dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep395/usrep395337/usrep395337.pdf"},"1964/86":{"facts":"After the United States broke diplomatic relations with Cuba in January 1961, the State Department removed Cuba from the area for which no passport was required and declared outstanding passports invalid for Cuba unless specially endorsed. Louis Zemel, a citizen holding a valid passport, asked to have it validated for travel to Cuba as a tourist, to satisfy his curiosity and become a better informed citizen. The request was denied twice because his purpose did not meet the standards for such travel. He then sued the Secretary of State and the Attorney General in federal district court in Connecticut.","procedure":"Zemel sought a declaration that he could travel to Cuba, that the Secretary's restrictions were invalid, and that the Passport Act of 1926 and § 215 of the Immigration and Nationality Act of 1952 were unconstitutional, plus an injunction. A three-judge district court, by a divided vote, granted the Secretary summary judgment and dismissed the action against the Attorney General. Zemel appealed directly to the Supreme Court.","question":"Is the Secretary of State statutorily authorized to refuse to validate United States passports for travel to Cuba, and if so, does that refusal violate the Fifth Amendment liberty to travel, the First Amendment, or the nondelegation doctrine? Must the Court also decide whether Zemel could be criminally prosecuted under § 215(b) for traveling in violation of an area restriction?","holding":"The Passport Act of 1926 authorizes the Executive to impose area restrictions such as the Cuba ban, and the restriction does not violate Zemel's constitutional rights. The Court declined to decide the reach or validity of the criminal provision, § 215(b), because that question needs a concrete factual setting.","reasoning":"The Court first held that the three-judge court was properly convened because the complaint attacked two federal statutes as invalid delegations and sought to enjoin them. On the merits, the Act's broad language, together with a long history of executive area restrictions before and after 1926 and Congress's leaving that authority untouched when it legislated on passports in 1952, showed congressional approval; Kent v. Dulles was distinguished because there the passport was denied for the applicant's beliefs or associations, not for foreign policy reasons affecting all citizens. The right to travel is part of Fifth Amendment liberty but can be restricted when needed, and Cuba-related dangers (subversion, the missile crisis, risk of international incidents) supported the restriction as resting on \"the weightiest considerations of national security\". The ban inhibits action, not First Amendment expression, since the right to speak and publish \"does not carry with it the unrestrained right to gather information\". Because foreign affairs call for a broader brush, and the Act authorizes only restrictions it could fairly be argued Congress adopted in light of prior administrative practice, there was no invalid delegation. The criminal-liability question was left open because the complaint did not say what travel Zemel planned or what charge might follow.","separate":"Justice Black dissented: Article I vests all legislative power in Congress, the President has no inherent power over passports, and the 1926 Act is an unconstitutional delegation of unbounded lawmaking power to the Secretary and President. Justice Douglas, joined by Justice Goldberg, dissented: the right to travel is peripheral to First Amendment freedoms of knowing and communicating, and Congress has not authorized an executive ban in narrowly drawn terms. Justice Goldberg dissented separately: the Executive has no inherent power to impose peacetime area restrictions, and the 1926 Act, whose history shows it was meant to centralize passport issuance, did not grant such authority; he would have reversed.","disposition":"Affirmed. Chief Justice Warren delivered the opinion of the Court; the opinion text does not state the vote count or list the Justices who joined it.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep381/usrep381001/usrep381001.pdf"},"1964/64":{"facts":"Robert Swain, a Black man, was indicted and convicted of rape in Talladega County, Alabama, and sentenced to death. Black men were 26% of males over 21 in the county, but jury panels since 1953 had averaged only 10 to 15% Black, and Black members had served on most grand juries. Petit venires averaged six to seven Black members, yet none had actually served on a petit jury since about 1950. In Swain's case eight Black venire members appeared; two were exempt and the prosecutor struck the other six.","procedure":"Swain moved to quash the indictment, strike the venire and declare the petit jury void, all alleging racial discrimination in jury selection. The trial court denied the motions and the Alabama Supreme Court affirmed the conviction. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did Alabama deny Swain equal protection through the disproportionately low number of Black people on jury panels, through the prosecutor's peremptory strikes of all Black venire members in his case, or through a county-wide pattern of striking Black jurors so that none ever serve on petit juries?","holding":"None of these showings established a violation of the Equal Protection Clause. A defendant is not entitled to proportional racial representation, purposeful discrimination must be proved, the prosecutor's use of peremptory strikes in a particular case is presumed proper, and Swain failed to carry his burden of proving the prosecutor's systematic use of strikes against Black jurors over time.","reasoning":"Purposeful exclusion of an identifiable group violates equal protection but may not be assumed. The Court held that an average Black share of 10 to 15% against 26% in the population did not prove purposeful discrimination, given that commissioners denied using racial considerations and nothing showed different standards; \"an imperfect system is not equivalent to purposeful discrimination based on race.\" The peremptory challenge has a long history, is used without stated reasons and often on group assumptions, so subjecting the prosecutor's strikes in a single case to equal protection scrutiny would change its nature, and the presumption is that strikes aim at a fair jury. A county-wide pattern in which the prosecutor, case after case, removes all Black jurors so that none ever serve might support a prima facie case, but the Court did not decide that. The record here did not show when, how often, and under what circumstances the prosecutor alone, rather than defense counsel, was responsible for strikes, so Swain, who bears the burden of proof, did not carry it. The rule that total exclusion by state officers supports an inference of discrimination cannot be applied mechanically because defense counsel also participate in peremptories.","separate":"Justice Harlan concurred, stressing that the Court reserves and does not decide the Part III question of a systematic-striking claim. Justice Black concurred in the result. Justice Goldberg, joined by Chief Justice Warren and Justice Douglas, dissented: proof that Black people are a substantial part of the eligible population and that none has served over a long period makes a prima facie case under Strauder, Norris and Patton that the State must rebut. He found state involvement in the record and would have reversed.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice White delivered the opinion of the Court; Justice Harlan concurred, Justice Black concurred in the result, and three Justices dissented as noted. The text does not list a full vote count for the opinion.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep380/usrep380202/usrep380202.pdf"},"1960/225":{"facts":"In October 1957 a Kansas City police lieutenant investigating allegedly obscene magazines filed sworn complaints in a Missouri circuit court against a wholesale magazine distributor and the operators of five newsstands, alleging that each kept obscene publications for sale. No magazine was filed with the complaints or shown to the judge, and the judge issued warrants that merely repeated the statutory language. Several police officers executed the warrants the same day, seizing about 11,000 copies of 280 publications that in their own judgment were obscene; the owners had no prior hearing.","procedure":"After a hearing the Missouri circuit judge denied the motions to quash and found 100 of the 280 items obscene (ordering them held as evidence and later destroyed) and ordered the other 180 returned. The Missouri Supreme Court sustained the procedures as applied, and the owners appealed to the U.S. Supreme Court under 28 U.S.C. § 1257(2).","question":"Did Missouri's procedures, as applied here to authorize police to search for and seize allegedly obscene publications before any judicial determination of obscenity, deny the appellants due process under the Fourteenth Amendment by failing to protect constitutionally protected expression?","holding":"Yes. A State may not adopt whatever procedures it pleases for dealing with obscenity without regard to the consequences for protected speech, and a mass seizure on the conclusory complaint of one officer, leaving the choice of what to seize to many officers, lacks the safeguards due process requires. The judgment sustaining condemnation of the 100 publications cannot stand.","reasoning":"The Court first held that the appeal was properly before it. It traced the history of general warrants and licensing in England and the Bill of Rights background to show that unrestricted search and seizure power has been an instrument for suppressing expression. Although obscenity is outside protected speech under Roth, the line between protected and unprotected speech is finely drawn, so procedures for suppressing obscenity must be adequate to avoid sweeping in protected material. Here the warrants issued on one officer's conclusory assertions without the judge examining any publication, and left the selection of items to officers who made ad hoc decisions in a short time; 180 items later found not obscene were withheld for over two months. Kingsley Books was distinguished because there the court had the named publication before it, the restraint reached only that work, and a prompt decision was required, whereas here all copies were physically removed before any adversary hearing and no time limit governed the decision. The Court did not reach whether the items were obscene or whether Missouri lacks all power to seize obscene material.","separate":"Justice Black, joined by Justice Douglas, concurred, reasoning that the warrant failed to describe the things to be seized as the Fourth Amendment requires, and that the Fourteenth Amendment makes the Fourth Amendment applicable to the States to the full extent of its terms, so the general warrant violated both.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded for further proceedings not inconsistent with the opinion. Opinion of the Court by Justice Brennan; the text does not state the vote count.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep367/usrep367717/usrep367717.pdf"},"1962/27":{"facts":"A union sought to pressure unionized-resistant Nebraska short-line motor carriers by getting larger unionized trunk-line carriers to refuse interline traffic from them, relying on \"hot cargo\" clauses in the trunk-line carriers' union contracts. The refusals disrupted service to and from interior Nebraska points. The short-line carriers formed Nebraska Short Line Carriers, Inc., which applied to the Interstate Commerce Commission for authority to operate as a common carrier between Nebraska and other States. Some trunk-line carriers, including Burlington and Santa Fe Trail, kept normal interline relations and would lose traffic to the new carrier.","procedure":"The Commission granted part of the application, finding serious inadequacies in service caused by the union-induced boycott, and said other remedies such as complaints did not alter the situation. Four months later Congress added § 8(e) to the National Labor Relations Act. A three-judge District Court sustained the order, and the carriers and a union appealed directly to the Supreme Court.","question":"Could the Interstate Commerce Commission grant additional operating authority to remedy service inadequacies caused solely by illegal refusals to interline, without findings and analysis justifying that remedy over a cease-and-desist order, and should the District Court have sent the matter back in light of the 1959 labor law amendment?","holding":"No. The Commission's order was an improvident exercise of its discretion because it made no findings or analysis justifying the choice of the certification remedy over complaint and cease-and-desist procedures, and a reviewing court may uphold an agency's discretionary order only on the grounds the agency itself stated. The District Court also should have vacated the order and remanded to the Commission in light of the new § 8(e).","reasoning":"The Court accepted the Commission's findings that service was disrupted but found fault with its choice of remedy. Where the only deficiency is illegal and discriminatory refusals to deal, which would not occur absent union pressure, the Commission's cease-and-desist and related powers bear heavily on whether certification is proper, so the choice \"must be rational and based upon conscious choice\" weighing the public interests involved. The Commission offered no findings or analysis for its choice, as the Administrative Procedure Act requires. Under Chenery, the Court refused to accept appellate counsel's after-the-fact argument that a cease-and-desist order would have been ineffective, and found no substantial evidence supporting that claim anyway. Section 8(e), enacted before the District Court ruled, raised serious questions about the legality of the boycott, so the changed circumstances called for remand. On remand the Commission must act with a discriminating awareness of effects on national labor policy, though the Court did not rule out certification in all cases.","separate":"Justice Black concurred in setting aside the order but dissented from the remand, arguing that the Commission lacked power to grant a permanent certificate here because that encroached on the Labor Board's domain, and that the proceedings were effectively moot. Justice Clark concurred in the result, agreeing the District Court should have vacated and remanded, but suggesting the issue may now be moot. Justice Goldberg, joined by the Chief Justice and Justices Douglas and Brennan, concurred, stating that the required careful analysis must lead, if any relief is warranted, to a limited cease-and-desist order rather than additional certification.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded to the District Court with instructions to set aside the Commission's order and remand to the Commission for further proceedings. Opinion of the Court by Justice White; the text does not state a vote count.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep371/usrep371156/usrep371156.pdf"},"1965/597":{"facts":"On November 6, 1962, Birmingham, Alabama held an election on whether to keep its commission form of government or adopt a mayor-council form. On election day the Birmingham Post-Herald carried an editorial by its editor, James E. Mills, strongly urging voters to adopt the mayor-council form. Mills was arrested on a complaint charging that publishing the editorial on election day violated § 285 of the Alabama Corrupt Practices Act, which makes it a crime to electioneer or solicit votes on election day for or against any candidate or proposition being voted on.","procedure":"The trial court sustained demurrers on the ground that the statute violated state and federal free speech and press guarantees. The Alabama Supreme Court reversed, holding the restriction within the field of reasonableness and remanding for trial. Mills appealed to the U.S. Supreme Court, and the State moved to dismiss for lack of a final judgment.","question":"Is the Alabama Supreme Court's judgment final under 28 U.S.C. § 1257, and may a State consistently with the First and Fourteenth Amendments make it a crime for a newspaper editor to publish an editorial on election day urging people to vote a certain way on issues before them?","holding":"The judgment was final for purposes of appeal because conviction on remand was inevitable given the state court's ruling and Mills' concession that he wrote and published the editorial. The statute as applied violates the First Amendment, and \"no test of reasonableness can save a state law from invalidation\" when it criminalizes a newspaper editor merely urging people how to vote in a publicly held election.","reasoning":"On jurisdiction, the Court reasoned that a remand trial would be only formal gestures leading to conviction and another appeal, an inexcusable delay and waste, so it followed Pope, Curry and Richfield Oil in finding the judgment final. On the merits, the Court said a major purpose of the First Amendment was to protect free discussion of governmental affairs, and that the press was chosen to serve as an antidote to abuses of official power. The statute silences the press when it can be most effective, and the Court called it difficult to conceive of \"a more obvious and flagrant abridgment\" of press freedom. It rejected the Alabama court's reasonableness rationale, noting that the law permits campaign charges until the day before the election but bars answering them on election day, the only time they can effectively be answered, so it does not even achieve its stated purpose. The Court noted that its holding does not concern a State's power to regulate conduct in and around the polls.","separate":"Justice Douglas, joined by Justice Brennan, concurred to explain why the judgment was final, stressing the chilling effect on election-day editorials in Alabama and the importance of the First Amendment rights at stake. Justice Harlan filed a separate opinion, concluding that the judgment was not final and the Court lacked jurisdiction, but agreeing on the merits that the decision below could not stand; he would rest reversal on the ground that the statute did not give Mills fair warning that this editorial was covered.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded for further proceedings not inconsistent with the opinion. Opinion of the Court by Justice Black; the text does not state a vote count (Justice Harlan dissented on jurisdiction).","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep384/usrep384214/usrep384214.pdf"},"1966/114":{"facts":"Benjamin Owens, a union member with high blood pressure, was discharged from Swift & Company's Kansas City packing plant in January 1960 for poor health after the company doctor found his blood pressure too high, despite outside doctors certifying him fit. The union filed a grievance and processed it into the fourth of five steps, and in February 1961 sent Owens to another doctor at union expense to gather better medical evidence. That examination did not support him, so the union's executive board voted not to take the grievance to arbitration, and Owens rejected a rehabilitation alternative and sued.","procedure":"Owens sued the union officers in a Missouri state court, alleging wrongful discharge and the union's arbitrary refusal to take his grievance to arbitration. A jury awarded him compensatory and punitive damages, but the trial judge set the verdict aside on the ground that the NLRB had exclusive jurisdiction, and the Court of Appeals affirmed. The Missouri Supreme Court reversed and reinstated the verdict, and the U.S. Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Is a state court's jurisdiction over an employee's suit against his union for breach of the duty of fair representation pre-empted by the NLRB's exclusive jurisdiction over unfair labor practices, and if not, was the union liable on this record and were the damages properly awarded?","holding":"State courts have jurisdiction over such suits, and the Garmon pre-emption doctrine does not apply. A union breaches its duty of fair representation only when its conduct toward a member is \"arbitrary, discriminatory, or in bad faith,\" and it does not breach the duty merely by settling a grievance short of arbitration. The evidence here showed no such breach, and damages attributable to the employer's breach of contract may not be charged to the union.","reasoning":"The Court reasoned that the duty of fair representation is grounded in federal statutes and was judicially developed, so the main justification for pre-emption (avoiding conflicting substantive rules developed by the Board) does not apply. Pre-emption would also leave employees without recourse where the General Counsel declines to issue a complaint, undermining the doctrine's purpose. Courts must in any event decide whether the union breached its duty in many § 301 suits against employers, where an employee barred from exhausting contractual remedies by the union's wrongful refusal may sue the employer, so it would make little sense to deny courts power to adjudicate and remedy the union's breach. On the merits, an individual employee has no absolute right to have his grievance arbitrated, because union discretion to settle grievances serves the contract system, and a verdict resting only on the merits of the discharge applied the wrong standard. The union had processed the grievance diligently, sought more medical evidence, and acted in good faith, so there was no breach. On remedy, liability should be apportioned between employer and union according to the damage caused by the fault of each.","separate":"Justice Fortas, joined by the Chief Justice and Justice Harlan, concurred in the result, arguing that a claim of breach of the duty of fair representation is an unfair labor practice within the NLRB's exclusive jurisdiction, though he agreed the judgment must be reversed on the standard and evidence. Justice Black dissented, arguing that the Court's ruling burdens employees suing their employers for breach of contract with proving the union acted arbitrarily or in bad faith, and that Owens, who had tried to exhaust the grievance procedure, should be able to sue Swift or the union should have a duty to take serious grievances to arbitration.","disposition":"Reversed. Opinion of the Court by Justice White; the text shows Justice Fortas concurring in the result (joined by the Chief Justice and Justice Harlan) and Justice Black dissenting.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep386/usrep386171/usrep386171.pdf"},"1967/949":{"facts":"Alabama, like other States, participates in the federally funded Aid to Families With Dependent Children program. In 1964 it adopted a \"substitute father\" regulation denying AFDC payments to the children of a mother who \"cohabits\" with any able-bodied man, whether or not he is the children's father, is legally obliged to support them, or in fact does so. Mrs. Smith and her four children had received AFDC for years, but in October 1966 their aid was terminated under the regulation because a Mr. Williams, who had a wife and nine children of his own, came to her home on weekends and had sexual relations with her. He was not the father of her children, was not legally obligated under Alabama law to support them, and did not.","procedure":"Mrs. Smith and her children brought a class action under 42 U.S.C. § 1983 against Alabama officials in federal district court. A three-judge court, without requiring exhaustion of state administrative remedies, held the regulation inconsistent with the Social Security Act and the Equal Protection Clause. The State appealed and the Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction.","question":"Is Alabama's substitute father regulation, which denies AFDC aid to children because their mother cohabits with a man who has no legal duty to support them, consistent with the federal Social Security Act's definition of \"dependent child\" and its requirement of aid to all eligible individuals?","holding":"No. Congress meant by \"parent\" in § 406(a) of the Act \"an individual who owed to the child a state-imposed legal duty of support,\" so Alabama may not disqualify a child based on a man with no such duty. The regulation is invalid, and applying it to deny aid to eligible children breaches the State's federal obligation to furnish aid with reasonable promptness to all eligible individuals.","reasoning":"The Court noted what was not at issue: States may set standards of need and benefit levels and may count actual contributions to a child's support. It rejected Alabama's first justification, discouraging immorality and illegitimacy, because the Flemming Ruling and the 1961 and 1962 amendments show Congress has decided such problems should be met through rehabilitative measures, not by punishing dependent children, so the regulation \"plainly conflicts with federal law and policy.\" On Alabama's second justification, treating informal couples like married ones, the Court said a child's father has a legal duty of support while an unrelated substitute father does not. From the legislative history, the repeated references to breadwinners, and other provisions of the Act that use \"parent\" in connection with enforcing support obligations, the Court concluded Congress intended \"parent\" to mean only persons with a legal duty of support. Having decided on statutory grounds, the Court did not reach the equal protection claim.","separate":"Justice Douglas concurred, preferring to reach the constitutional question: he argued that the regulation punishes mothers for nonmarital sexual relations at the expense of needy children, that the mother's immorality has no rational connection to the children's need, and that it denies equal protection, as with the illegitimate children in Levy v. Louisiana. He also noted a long-standing administrative construction approving man-in-the-house provisions and appended a list of States with such policies.","disposition":"Affirmed. Opinion of the Court by Chief Justice Warren; the text does not state a vote count, and shows Justice Douglas concurring.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep392/usrep392309/usrep392309.pdf"},"1963/11":{"facts":"Nico Jacobellis managed a motion picture theater in Cleveland Heights, Ohio. He was convicted on two counts of possessing and exhibiting an obscene film under an Ohio obscenity statute and was fined $500 and $2,000, with workhouse confinement if the fines were unpaid. The film was a French picture called \"Les Amants\" (\"The Lovers\"), about a bored wife who leaves her husband and family for a young archaeologist. The State's objections rested almost entirely on an explicit love scene in the last reel.","procedure":"A three-judge court convicted Jacobellis after he waived a jury. An intermediate appellate court and the Ohio Supreme Court affirmed. The Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction of the appeal, then restored the case to the calendar for reargument.","question":"Did the state courts properly find the film obscene, and therefore outside the First and Fourteenth Amendment protection for free expression, and what standard governs that determination, including whether the community standards are local or national?","holding":"The Court reversed, holding that the film is not obscene under the Roth standard and the conviction cannot stand. In the Brennan opinion (joined only by Justice Goldberg), the Court must make an independent constitutional judgment on whether material is protected, and the Roth test is to be applied by a national standard rather than a local one.","reasoning":"Justice Brennan wrote that motion pictures are protected expression but obscenity is not, so deciding whether a work is obscene is itself a question of constitutional law that the Court cannot leave to a \"sufficient evidence\" review. He adhered to the Roth test (average person, contemporary community standards, dominant theme appealing to prurient interest) and stressed that material is obscene only if it is utterly without redeeming social importance and goes substantially beyond customary limits of candor. He read Judge Learned Hand's \"community\" as society at large, not a town or county, and reasoned that a local standard would deny some areas access to material acceptable elsewhere and would chill distribution everywhere. He concluded that \"It is, after all, a national Constitution we are expounding.\" He added that the interest in protecting children does not justify total suppression, since this conviction rested on exhibition to the public at large. Having viewed the film in light of the trial record, and noting favorable reviews in national publications, he found it not obscene.","separate":"Justice White concurred in the judgment without an opinion. Justice Black, joined by Justice Douglas, concurred because any conviction for exhibiting a motion picture abridges freedom of the press under the First Amendment as applied to the States. Justice Stewart concurred, concluding that criminal obscenity laws are limited to hard-core pornography, and wrote \"But I know it when I see it, and the motion picture involved in this case is not that.\" Justice Goldberg concurred, adding that the love scene was fragmentary and fleeting and that Joseph Burstyn v. Wilson requires reversal because there is no justification for an exception to the rule of freedom of expression. Chief Justice Warren, joined by Justice Clark, dissented: he would keep the Roth test, read its community standards as local rather than national, and review state findings only for sufficient evidence, finding enough here to affirm. Justice Harlan dissented, adhering to his view that States have more latitude than the federal government over obscenity and should be limited only by a rationality test, and he would affirm.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Brennan announced the judgment of the Court and delivered an opinion joined by Justice Goldberg; Justices White, Black, Douglas, Stewart and Goldberg also supported reversal in separate writings, and Chief Justice Warren, Justice Clark and Justice Harlan dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep378/usrep378184/usrep378184.pdf"},"1964/40":{"facts":"In December 1963, Texas officers searched John Stanford's San Antonio home, where he also ran a mail-order book business, under a warrant issued under the Texas Suppression Act. The warrant authorized seizure of \"books, records, pamphlets, cards, receipts, lists, memoranda, pictures, recordings and other written instruments concerning the Communist Party of Texas.\" Over more than four hours the officers took about 2,000 items, including books from his business stock and personal library and private papers such as his marriage certificate and insurance policies. They found no Communist Party records or party lists and dues payments.","procedure":"Stanford asked the magistrate who issued the warrant to annul it and return the property, raising federal constitutional claims. The motion was denied without opinion, and the parties agreed that the denial was final and not reviewable under Texas law. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does a warrant that authorizes seizure of all books, records, and other written materials concerning the Communist Party of Texas satisfy the Fourth Amendment's requirement, applicable to the States through the Fourteenth Amendment, that warrants particularly describe the things to be seized?","holding":"No. The warrant was a general warrant of the kind the Fourth Amendment was meant to forbid. The particularity requirement must be met with the most scrupulous exactitude when the things to be seized are books and the basis for seizure is the ideas they contain.","reasoning":"The Court noted that the Fourth Amendment's protections are enforced against the States through the Fourteenth. It traced the history of general warrants, including the writs of assistance, Wilkes v. Wood, and Entick v. Carrington, and observed that this history was largely one of conflict between the Crown and the press. From that history the Court concluded that the particularity requirement must be applied with \"the most scrupulous exactitude\" when books are the targets, because a lesser standard would not be faithful to First Amendment freedoms. The Court said it need not decide how it would treat a description of weapons, narcotics, or whiskey. This warrant instead covered literary material, and the officers' wide-ranging seizure showed the danger of leaving the choice to their discretion. The Court called the warrant's \"indiscriminate sweep\" constitutionally intolerable and rested its decision on this one ground without reaching the petitioner's other claims.","disposition":"The order denying the motion to annul the warrant was vacated and the cause remanded for further proceedings not inconsistent with the opinion. Justice Stewart delivered the opinion of the Court; the opinion text does not state a vote count or any separate opinion.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep379/usrep379476/usrep379476.pdf"},"1966/68":{"facts":"The three cases challenge Texas's recidivist (habitual-criminal) statutes, which enhance punishment for people with prior convictions. Under the procedure then in use, the indictment alleged the defendant's prior convictions and the State proved them in the same trial in which the jury decided guilt on the new charge. The court told the jury not to consider the priors on guilt or innocence. Spencer was indicted for murder, with a prior malice-murder conviction alleged, and sentenced to death. Bell (robbery) and Reed (burglary) were sentenced to life as repeat offenders after juries heard proof of earlier convictions.","procedure":"Spencer (No. 68) and Bell (No. 69) came from the Texas Court of Criminal Appeals, which affirmed their convictions. Reed (No. 70) came from the Fifth Circuit, which affirmed denial of habeas corpus. The Court dismissed Spencer's appeal, treated it as a petition for certiorari, and granted it because the courts of appeals had divided on the issue.","question":"Does the Due Process Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment forbid a State from telling the jury about a defendant's prior convictions, with limiting instructions, in the same trial that decides guilt on the current charge, when the State uses the convictions to enforce a recidivist statute?","holding":"No. Texas's one-stage procedure, in which prior convictions are put before the jury with a limiting instruction, does not violate due process. The Court was unwilling to hold that the Constitution requires a two-stage trial for recidivist cases.","reasoning":"The Court noted that no one argued that recidivist statutes are themselves invalid or that juries may not fix punishment. It reasoned that evidence of prior crimes is routinely admitted for other purposes, such as intent, identity, impeachment, or rebutting character evidence, and that in each case the possibility of prejudice is accepted because it is outweighed by a valid state purpose and limited by instructions and judicial discretion. A rule that such evidence is unconstitutional whenever it may prejudice would threaten much of state evidence law and practices like joint trials. It said the Court has never acted as a \"rule-making organ for the promulgation of state rules of criminal procedure.\" It distinguished Jackson v. Denno as protecting a specific federal right, the right not to have an involuntary confession relied on, rather than resting on a general fairness theory. Because recidivism is common and States use a variety of procedures, the Court held that a two-stage jury trial, though perhaps the fairest, is not compelled by the Fourteenth Amendment.","separate":"Justice Stewart concurred. He said he would find the newer recidivist procedures, including Texas's own since 1966, far superior, but that the only question was whether the old procedure falls below the Fourteenth Amendment's minimum. Chief Justice Warren, joined by Justice Fortas, dissented in Nos. 68 and 69 and concurred in No. 70. He argued that prior convictions are relevant only to punishment, so presenting them to the jury before guilt is decided serves no legitimate state interest and prejudices the accused. He would have required at least that a defendant be allowed to stipulate to the priors to keep them from the jury. He would apply that rule only to nonfinal convictions, and so would affirm No. 70, the habeas case. Justice Brennan, joined by Justice Douglas, dissented. He joined the Chief Justice in reversing Nos. 68 and 69 and would also reverse No. 70, applying the rule retroactively.","disposition":"In No. 68, the appeal was dismissed, certiorari was granted, and the judgment was affirmed. The judgments in Nos. 69 and 70 were also affirmed. Justice Harlan delivered the opinion of the Court; the opinion text does not state a vote count and does not show who joined him.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep385/usrep385554/usrep385554.pdf"},"1969/33":{"facts":"Little Hunting Park, a Virginia nonstock corporation, operated a community park and playground for residents of an area in Fairfax County. A membership share let the holder's family use the facilities, and under the bylaws a shareholder who rented out his home could assign the share to his tenant, subject to board approval. Paul Sullivan leased a house to T. R. Freeman, Jr., a Black man, and assigned him his membership share. The board refused to approve the assignment because Freeman was a Negro, and it then expelled Sullivan after he protested. Sullivan and Freeman sued under 42 U.S.C. sections 1981 and 1982. Freeman had since left the area, so his claim was for damages only.","procedure":"The Virginia trial court dismissed the complaints, finding that the park was a private social club. The Supreme Court of Appeals of Virginia refused the appeals for failure to give opposing counsel reasonable notice of tendering the transcript under its Rule 5:1. This Court vacated and remanded in light of Jones v. Mayer Co. The Virginia court again said it lacked jurisdiction because of the rule violation, and this Court again granted certiorari.","question":"Did Virginia's procedural notice rule bar review, and if not, did the park's refusal to approve the assignment of the membership share violate 42 U.S.C. section 1982, and what relief is available to Sullivan and Freeman?","holding":"The notice rule was not a bar to review, because it is \"more properly deemed discretionary than jurisdictional.\" Refusing to approve the assignment because of race interfered with Freeman's right to lease and violated section 1982, Sullivan has standing to sue, and the petitioners may recover damages under federal law.","reasoning":"The Court found that Virginia had not applied its notice rule so consistently that it amounted to self-denial of power to hear the federal claim, so certiorari was proper. On the merits, relying on Jones v. Mayer Co., the Court held that section 1982 reaches private discrimination and is authorized by the Thirteenth Amendment. It found nothing on this record to support the view that the park was a private social club: it was open to every white person in the area, with no selective element other than race, and so was \"a device functionally comparable to a racially restrictive covenant.\" The membership share was part of the lease, since Freeman paid part of his rent for it, and the right to lease is protected against third parties as well as lessors. Under Barrows v. Jackson, Sullivan, punished for championing Freeman's cause, could sue as the effective adversary. The 1964 and 1968 Civil Rights Acts did not displace section 1982, state courts with general injunctive power can enforce it, and a statutory right implies the needed remedies, including compensatory damages under federal standards drawn from federal or state sources as best serves the statute's policies.","separate":"Justice Harlan, joined by the Chief Justice and Justice White, dissented. He agreed that there was no adequate state ground barring review but found the majority's reasoning unclear. He would have dismissed the writ as improvidently granted, since the Fair Housing Title of the 1968 Act now provides a detailed scheme, and he objected to extending the vague section 1982 and implying a damages remedy. He also argued that the Court failed to explain the standard for Sullivan's rights, that disputed facts about his expulsion might require a remand, and that the majority overlooked the problems of a state court providing remedies for a federal right.","disposition":"Reversed. The opinion of the Court was written by Justice Douglas and announced by Justice Black. The opinion text does not state a vote count; Justice Harlan's dissent was joined by the Chief Justice and Justice White.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep396/usrep396229/usrep396229.pdf"},"1962/600":{"facts":"White was arrested on a murder charge in May 1960 and brought before a Maryland magistrate for a preliminary hearing, which was postponed until August 9, 1960. He had no lawyer at that hearing and pleaded guilty. Counsel was appointed in September 1960, and at the formal arraignment in November he pleaded not guilty and not guilty by reason of insanity. At his trial the guilty plea entered at the preliminary hearing was introduced in evidence, and he was convicted and sentenced to death.","procedure":"The Maryland Court of Appeals affirmed, reasoning that under Maryland law the preliminary hearing was not a critical stage and no counsel was required or plea necessary. The Supreme Court granted certiorari limited to the point of law raised in Hamilton v. Alabama.","question":"Did the absence of counsel when the accused entered a guilty plea at a Maryland preliminary hearing, a plea later used against him at trial, violate the Due Process Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment under Hamilton v. Alabama?","holding":"Yes. Whatever the usual role of the Maryland preliminary hearing, it was as critical a stage here as arraignment was in Hamilton because he entered a plea without counsel, so Hamilton governs and no showing of prejudice is required.","reasoning":"The Court noted that the Maryland court distinguished Hamilton because Alabama arraignment is a critical stage while Maryland's preliminary hearing supposedly was not. The Court answered that, whatever the hearing's normal function, in this case petitioner entered a plea before the magistrate at a time when he had no counsel, which made the stage as critical as arraignment in Alabama. It repeated Hamilton's statement that it does not inquire whether prejudice resulted: \"Only the presence of counsel could have enabled this accused to know all the defenses available to him and to plead intelligently.\" A footnote adds that the failure to object at trial does not matter because Hamilton's rationale does not depend on a showing of prejudice.","disposition":"Reversed, in a per curiam opinion. The text does not state a vote count or name individual Justices in the majority.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep373/usrep373059/usrep373059.pdf"},"1967/339":{"facts":"Petitioners brought a class action under Title II of the Civil Rights Act of 1964 to enjoin racial discrimination at five drive-in restaurants and a sandwich shop owned and operated by the respondents in South Carolina. The District Court found that each restaurant affected commerce and that Black patrons had been discriminated against at all six. It wrongly concluded, however, that Title II does not cover drive-in restaurants like these, and so enjoined discrimination only at the sandwich shop.","procedure":"The Court of Appeals reversed as to the drive-ins. On the attorney's fee provision, Section 204(b), it directed the District Court to award counsel fees only to the extent respondents' defenses had been advanced \"for purposes of delay and not in good faith.\" The Supreme Court granted certiorari to decide whether that subjective standard properly serves the fee provision.","question":"Under the counsel-fee provision of Title II of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, may a prevailing plaintiff recover attorney's fees only when the defendant's defenses were advanced for delay and not in good faith?","holding":"No. One who succeeds in obtaining an injunction under Title II should ordinarily recover an attorney's fee unless special circumstances would render such an award unjust.","reasoning":"The Court reasoned that when the Act was passed enforcement was expected to depend in part on private litigation. A Title II plaintiff cannot recover damages and, if he wins an injunction, he acts as a \"private attorney general\" vindicating a policy Congress ranked of the highest priority. If successful plaintiffs routinely bore their own fees, few aggrieved persons could bring suit, so Congress provided for fees to encourage such suits and not merely to penalize bad-faith defenses. Fees against defendants who litigate in bad faith were already available without a new statute, which shows Congress meant something broader. The Court found no special circumstances here, noting that respondents' defenses were patently frivolous.","disposition":"Judgment of the Court of Appeals modified, and as modified, affirmed, per curiam, with the District Court to include reasonable counsel fees as costs on remand. Justice Marshall took no part. The text does not state a vote count.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep390/usrep390400/usrep390400.pdf"},"1961/59":{"facts":"An American Airlines plane crashed in Missouri on a flight from Tulsa, Oklahoma, to New York, killing its passengers. The airline paid or tendered the $15,000 maximum under the Missouri Wrongful Death Act to each petitioner, who are the passengers' personal representatives. They sued the United States under the Federal Tort Claims Act in an Oklahoma federal court, seeking more under the Oklahoma Wrongful Death Act, which has no cap, on the theory that the Federal Aviation Agency negligently failed to enforce regulations against practices at the airline's Tulsa overhaul depot.","procedure":"The District Court dismissed the complaints for failure to state a claim, holding that Oklahoma's act could not apply extraterritorially and alternatively that Oklahoma's conflicts rule would point to Missouri law. The Court of Appeals for the Tenth Circuit affirmed by a divided vote, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari to resolve a conflict among the circuits.","question":"In a Federal Tort Claims Act suit where the negligent act occurs in one State and the injury in another, which law governs: the internal law of the place of the act, the whole law (including choice-of-law rules) of that place, or the internal law of the place of injury?","holding":"The Act requires federal courts to look to the law of the State where the negligent act or omission occurred, and that means the whole law of that State, including its choice-of-law rules. Because Oklahoma would apply the law of the place of injury, Missouri's statute controlled and petitioners stated no further claim.","reasoning":"The Court found the legislative history unhelpful because Congress did not consider choice-of-law problems. It held that the words \"the law of the place where the act or omission occurred\" plainly refer to the place of the negligent act, rejecting the place-of-injury alternative. On whether that means internal or whole law, it reasoned that a statute must be read as a whole in light of its purpose, and that this Act builds on state-defined legal relationships. Applying the whole law lets federal courts treat the United States like a private individual under like circumstances and gives flexibility as States depart from the older place-of-injury rule. The Act's specific departures from state law do not show that Congress meant a separate federal conflicts rule. The Court accepted the lower courts' reading of Oklahoma decisions that Oklahoma applies the law of the place of injury, so the Missouri statute controlled.","disposition":"Affirmed. Chief Justice Warren delivered the opinion of the Court; the text shows no separate opinions and does not state a vote count.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep369/usrep369001/usrep369001.pdf"},"1961/50":{"facts":"A collective bargaining contract between Lucas Flour Co. and the Teamsters local reserved the employer's right to discharge any employee whose work was unsatisfactory and provided for final and binding arbitration of differences between employer and employee. It had no explicit no-strike clause for such disputes. The employer discharged an employee, Welsch, after he damaged a fork-lift truck, and the union struck for eight days to force his rehiring. The dispute was later arbitrated and the board ruled that his discharge was justified. The employer sued the union for business losses caused by the strike.","procedure":"The King County Superior Court in Washington awarded the employer $6,501.60, and Department One of the Washington Supreme Court affirmed, applying state contract law and finding the strike breached the contract. The union did not seek rehearing en banc. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does state or federal law govern a damages suit over a strike under a collective bargaining agreement within Section 301 of the Labor Management Relations Act, and does a strike over a dispute the contract sends to binding arbitration violate the agreement even without an explicit no-strike clause?","holding":"Federal law governs: \"incompatible doctrines of local law must give way to principles of federal labor law.\" Under federal law, a strike over a dispute the agreement commits to final and binding arbitration violates the agreement even without an express no-strike clause, so the judgment is affirmed.","reasoning":"The Court first held that it had jurisdiction because Washington's system treats a Department's decision as the State's final judgment unless rehearing is sought, unlike the Missouri scheme in Gorman. It held that Section 301 did not strip state courts of jurisdiction, following Dowd Box, and that Garmon pre-emption is not relevant to a contract suit. It then reasoned that the subject matter of Section 301 calls for uniform law: differing state and federal meanings of contract terms would disrupt the negotiation and administration of agreements and discourage agreement to arbitration, which would frustrate federal labor policy. On the merits, the contract bound both parties to arbitrate the discharge dispute, and several courts of appeals and the NLRB had treated a strike over an arbitrable dispute as a violation. Holding otherwise would offend traditional contract principles and the policy of promoting arbitration in place of economic warfare. The Court rejected the state court's reasoning that any strike during the agreement is a breach, and confined its holding to disputes the parties agreed to arbitrate.","separate":"Justice Black dissented. He argued that the Court rewrote the contract by implying a no-strike promise from an agreement to arbitrate. The contract contained an express no-strike sentence for one arbitration provision but none for the provision covering the discharge dispute, so he said the parties deliberately omitted it, and the implication had no support in the record. He would reverse and leave the matter to the collective bargaining table.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Stewart delivered the opinion of the Court; Justice Black dissented. The text does not state a vote count.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep369/usrep369095/usrep369095.pdf"},"1962/150":{"facts":"Silver and his firm, two Texas over-the-counter broker-dealers who were not members of the New York Stock Exchange, had direct private telephone wires to a number of New York Exchange member firms, which they needed to trade effectively. Under Exchange rules the member firms applied for approval of the wires and received temporary approval. In February 1959, without notice to petitioners, the Exchange disapproved the applications and ordered the members to remove the wires. Silver sought an explanation and was told it was Exchange policy not to disclose reasons. Petitioners say their business fell sharply or ended as a result.","procedure":"Petitioners sued the Exchange in federal district court for an injunction and treble damages under the Sherman and Clayton Acts. The District Court granted summary judgment and an injunction as to the wires, holding the removal a per se Sherman Act violation. The Second Circuit reversed, holding that the Exchange was exempt from the Sherman Act because it was exercising a power the Securities Exchange Act required of it. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 impliedly exempt a stock exchange from the antitrust laws, or otherwise justify the Exchange in ordering members to cut off private wires to a nonmember without notice, a statement of reasons, or a hearing?","holding":"The Act does not create a total exemption from the antitrust laws, but particular acts of exchange self-regulation within the Act's scope and purposes may be justified against an antitrust claim. Here, the collective removal of the wires without notice and a chance to be heard exceeded the Exchange's authority under the Act and was therefore an unjustified violation of § 1 of the Sherman Act.","reasoning":"Justice Goldberg first found that the collective action was, in simple terms, \"a group boycott\" that deprived petitioners of a service they needed to compete, which would be a per se Sherman Act violation absent other justification. The Court agreed that the Exchange's rules on wire connections were germane to its duty of self-regulation under sections 6(b) and 6(d) of the Act. But because repeals by implication are not favored, an exemption is implied only to the minimum extent necessary to make the Act work. The Act gives the Commission no authority to review particular applications of exchange rules, so nothing in the scheme performs the antitrust function of guarding against unjustified competitive injury, and antitrust review is compatible with the Act. Self-regulation is justified only insofar as it serves the Act's aims, and no policy of the Act is served by denying notice and a hearing; such procedures help prevent abuse and aid judicial review. Because the Exchange acted without them, it did not reach the threshold of justification, and the Court did not examine the reasons the Exchange later gave.","separate":"Justice Clark concurred in the result on the grounds stated in the District Court's opinion and the dissent in the Court of Appeals. Justice Stewart, joined by Justice Harlan, dissented, arguing that the Court had converted the case into one about due process notice and hearing, which was not briefed or argued, and that the antitrust laws should not be used to enforce fair procedures under an unrelated statute; he would hold that the Exchange Act removes antitrust liability for action taken in good faith to carry out the Exchange's statutory duty of self-regulation, and would vacate and remand.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded for further proceedings. Justice Goldberg delivered the opinion of the Court; Justice Clark concurred in the result; Justice Stewart, joined by Justice Harlan, dissented. The opinion text does not state a vote count.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep373/usrep373341/usrep373341.pdf"},"1964/18":{"facts":"On the afternoon of November 10, 1961, Cleveland police officers ordered Beck, who was driving, to pull over. They had neither an arrest nor a search warrant. They arrested him and searched his car, finding nothing of interest, then took him to a police station, searched his person, and found clearing house (numbers game) slips in his sock. The arresting officer testified only that he had a police picture of Beck, knew what he looked like and that Beck had a record on clearing house matters, and had unspecified \"information\" and \"reports\" from someone he did not identify.","procedure":"Beck was charged with possession of clearing house slips under an Ohio statute and moved to suppress the slips as the product of an unreasonable search and seizure. The trial court overruled the motion, and he was convicted. The Ohio Court of Appeals and, over two dissents, the Ohio Supreme Court affirmed, finding the search valid as incident to a lawful arrest. The U.S. Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did the officers have probable cause to arrest Beck without a warrant, so that the search of his person as incident to that arrest, and the admission of the slips, were consistent with the Fourth and Fourteenth Amendments?","holding":"No. On this record the officers did not have probable cause for the arrest, so the arrest and the search incident to it were invalid and the slips should have been suppressed.","reasoning":"Justice Stewart assumed that the search was within the permissible scope of a search incident to a lawful arrest, so its validity depended on whether the arrest was lawful, that is, whether at that moment the facts known to the officers and reasonably trustworthy information were sufficient to warrant a prudent person in believing Beck had committed or was committing an offense. The Court made an independent examination of the record, noting that the trial court made no findings. The record showed only that the officer knew Beck's appearance and prior record and that unnamed informants had told him something unspecified; it showed no indication of what the informant said or why it was credible. The Court distinguished Draper v. United States, where a named, reliable informant gave detailed information that was partly corroborated, and said the record here did not contain \"a single objective fact\" supporting a belief that Beck was engaged in crime. Knowledge of a prior record alone cannot justify arrest, or anyone with a record could be arrested at will, and the standards for a warrantless arrest cannot be less stringent than for a warrant. The officers' good faith is not enough.","separate":"Justice Clark, joined by Justice Black, dissented, arguing that the Ohio Supreme Court had determined facts, including an informer's tip that Beck would be in a certain place and the confirmation of that tip, that were supported by the record and should be respected, so he would affirm. Justice Harlan dissented separately, arguing that the state court's finding that an informer predicted Beck's presence at a certain place and time was a reasonable inference from the officer's testimony, that the Court should defer to state inferential findings dependent on on-the-spot considerations, and that the question had been raised for the first time by the Court itself.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Stewart delivered the opinion of the Court; Justice Clark (joined by Justice Black) and Justice Harlan dissented. The opinion text does not state a vote count.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep379/usrep379089/usrep379089.pdf"},"1967/55":{"facts":"Two unmasked men robbed a federally insured savings and loan association in Chicago in the afternoon, and five bank employees saw them. FBI agents who searched a house that day found a suitcase holding items linked to the robbery. The next day the agents separately showed the five employees a handful of snapshots, mostly group photos of Andrews, Simmons and others, and each employee picked out Simmons. Three employees later identified Garrett from photographs as the second robber. Before trial Garrett moved to suppress the suitcase and, to show standing, testified that he owned clothing in it and that it resembled one he had owned; that testimony was later admitted against him at trial.","procedure":"Simmons, Garrett and Andrews were convicted of the robbery in federal district court. The Seventh Circuit affirmed as to Simmons and Garrett but reversed Andrews' conviction for insufficient evidence. The Supreme Court granted certiorari as to Simmons and Garrett.","question":"Was the pretrial photographic identification of Simmons so suggestive that it denied due process or required reversal under the Court's supervisory power? Did the Jencks Act or the trial court's discretion require production of the photographs? May a defendant's testimony given to establish standing on a Fourth Amendment suppression motion be used against him at trial on the issue of guilt?","holding":"Each photographic identification case is judged on its own facts, and a conviction will be set aside only if the procedure was “so impermissibly suggestive as to give rise to a very substantial likelihood of irreparable misidentification”; that standard was not met as to Simmons. The Jencks Act did not reach the photographs, and refusing to order their production was not an abuse of discretion as to Simmons. When a defendant testifies in support of a Fourth Amendment suppression motion, that testimony may not be admitted against him at trial on the issue of guilt unless he makes no objection.","reasoning":"The Court declined to forbid photographic identification, noting its wide use and the protection offered by cross-examination, and adopted a totality-of-the-circumstances test in line with Stovall v. Denno. Using photographs was justified here because a serious felony had occurred, the robbers were at large, and the FBI had to learn quickly whether it was on the right track. The risk of misidentification was small: the bank was well lit, the robbers were unmasked, the witnesses saw the photographs the next day, each viewed them alone, and no one suggested which person was suspected. None identified Andrews, and all five stood by their identification of Simmons at trial. The photographs were obtained only after the witnesses gave their written statements, so they were not part of those statements under the Jencks Act. On Garrett, the Court reasoned that his testimony was needed to establish standing and was an integral part of his Fourth Amendment claim. Allowing its later use put him to a choice between that claim and his privilege against self-incrimination, and the Court found “intolerable that one constitutional right should have to be surrendered in order to assert another.”","separate":"Justice Black concurred in part and dissented in part. He agreed with affirming Simmons' conviction, but thought the due process claim frivolous and rejected the Court's totality-of-the-circumstances approach and its reliance on a supervisory power. He dissented from reversing Garrett's conviction, arguing that a truthful statement made at a suppression hearing is voluntary, that the Fifth Amendment privilege was validly waived, and that the rule lets defendants contradict their earlier sworn testimony. Justice White concurred in Parts I and II of the Court's opinion and dissented as to Garrett for substantially the reasons Justice Black gave.","disposition":"Affirmed as to Simmons; reversed as to Garrett and remanded to the Court of Appeals for further proceedings. Justice Harlan delivered the opinion of the Court. Justice Marshall took no part. Justices Black and White concurred in part and dissented in part; the text does not state a vote count.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep390/usrep390377/usrep390377.pdf"},"1964/49":{"facts":"Appellant Cox led a group of about 2,000 students in a demonstration across the street from the Baton Rouge courthouse, which also held the jail, to protest the arrest of 23 students the day before. The demonstrators stood on the far-side sidewalk, 101 feet from the courthouse steps, where city police officials had placed them and blocked off the area. Cox was charged under a Louisiana statute forbidding anyone, with intent to obstruct justice or influence a judge, juror, witness or court officer, to picket or parade \"in or near\" a courthouse. The charge rested on the same events as the breach-of-the-peace and obstructing-public-passages charges decided in No. 24, and Cox was sentenced to the maximum of one year and a $5,000 fine, cumulative with those penalties.","procedure":"The Louisiana Supreme Court affirmed the conviction. Cox appealed to the U.S. Supreme Court, arguing the statute was unconstitutional on its face and as applied, and probable jurisdiction was noted.","question":"Does the Louisiana courthouse-picketing statute violate the First and Fourteenth Amendments on its face or as applied to Cox, and may Cox be convicted for demonstrating at a spot that officials had told him he could use?","holding":"The statute is a valid regulation of conduct, not pure speech, and is not invalid on its face, and there was enough evidence of intent. But Cox's conviction cannot stand: officials in effect told him a demonstration at that place was not \"near\" the courthouse, and under Raley v. Ohio the Due Process Clause forbids convicting him for doing what they told him he could do.","reasoning":"The Court first found the statute a \"precise, narrowly drawn regulatory statute\" aimed at picketing and parading, serving the State's legitimate interest in keeping the judicial process free of outside pressure, and distinguished the contempt cases involving newspaper comment as involving pure speech. Even assuming a clear-and-present-danger test applied, demonstrations before a courthouse may be barred on a legislative judgment that they threaten the judicial process. The Court found direct evidence of intent in the demonstrators' stated aim of protesting the arrests, and said intent may also be inferred from circumstances. The word \"near\" gave officials a limited on-the-spot discretion to say how close a demonstration may be, and the record showed the police chief and other officials told Cox he could demonstrate across the street. Convicting him would be \"an indefensible sort of entrapment\" like that in Raley. The sheriff's later dispersal order did not remove that protection, because it rested on the officials' mistaken view that Cox's remarks threatened a breach of the peace, not on any time or place limit.","separate":"Justice Black (concurring in No. 24, dissenting in No. 49) would uphold the conviction: the State may protect judges and court officers from crowds, the record shows Cox's group meant to influence court officials, a police chief cannot authorize violation of the criminal law, and any permission was limited and later revoked. Justice Clark (concurring in No. 24, dissenting in No. 49) also dissented, rejecting the idea that an administrative determination of \"near\" was made and the Raley entrapment theory, and warning against mob pressure on courts; he also said the statute's labor-union exemption made it invalid under the equal protection reasoning of Justice Black. Justice White, joined by Justice Harlan, concurred in part and dissented in part, agreeing with Justice Black's dissent on No. 49.","disposition":"Reversed; the judgment of the Louisiana Supreme Court was reversed. Justice Goldberg delivered the opinion of the Court; the opinion text does not state a vote count. Justices Black and Clark dissented in No. 49, and Justice White, joined by Justice Harlan, dissented as to No. 49.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep379/usrep379559/usrep379559.pdf"},"1964/149":{"facts":"R. Wright Armstrong, Jr. and his wife were divorced in Texas in 1959; she received custody of their daughter and he was ordered to pay $50 a month in child support. After she married Salvatore Manzo in 1960, the Manzos petitioned in 1962 to let Manzo adopt the child. Texas law required the natural father's written consent unless he had not contributed substantially to support for two years, in which case the juvenile court judge's consent could substitute. Mrs. Manzo filed an affidavit alleging nonsupport, the juvenile judge consented, and the adoption petition was filed the same day, without any notice to Armstrong although the Manzos knew where he lived. The adoption decree was entered, and Armstrong learned of it only afterward.","procedure":"Armstrong moved in the trial court to set aside the decree for lack of notice; the court held a hearing at which he offered evidence of his support, then denied the motion and confirmed the decree. The Texas Court of Civil Appeals affirmed, holding the lack of notice was cured by that hearing, and the Texas Supreme Court refused review. The U.S. Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did failing to notify a father of pending adoption proceedings deny him due process of law, and did the later hearing on his motion to set aside the decree cure that defect?","holding":"Failure to give the father notice of the adoption proceedings violated due process, and the later hearing did not cure the defect. Only setting the decree aside and considering the case anew would have given him the hearing the Constitution requires.","reasoning":"The Court said that lack of notice violated \"the most rudimentary demands of due process of law\", relying on Mullane's requirement that deprivation of liberty or property by adjudication be preceded by notice and an opportunity for a hearing suited to the case, especially where a legitimate parent is permanently deprived of all that parenthood implies. The Texas court held the later hearing cured the problem, but the Supreme Court disagreed. With timely notice, the Manzos as moving parties would have borne the burden of proving why the father's consent was not required. Instead Armstrong faced an adverse decree and had to prove affirmatively that he had supported his child, and the burden of proof may be decisive. The opportunity to be heard must be given at a meaningful time and in a meaningful manner, and only granting his motion would have \"wiped the slate clean.\"","disposition":"Reversed and remanded for further proceedings not inconsistent with the opinion. Justice Stewart delivered the opinion of the Court; the opinion text shows no separate opinions and does not state a vote count.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep380/usrep380545/usrep380545.pdf"},"1965/318":{"facts":"Hawaii's Constitution gave three small counties 15 of the 25 state senate seats, while Oahu, with 79% of the state's population, got 10. The house of 51 was apportioned by the Governor on the basis of registered voters, giving Oahu 36 seats, with many multi-member districts. Residents of Honolulu sued in federal district court claiming the apportionment violated the Equal Protection Clause under Reynolds v. Sims. The Governor intervened as a plaintiff, and legislators intervened as defendants.","procedure":"A three-judge District Court held the senate provisions invalid but upheld the house apportionment, and ordered the legislature to submit a constitutional convention question to voters, later modified to require an interim senate plan based on registered voters. When the legislature passed a plan giving Oahu 19 senate seats in five multi-member districts, the court rejected it for lacking single-member districts and reinstated the convention order. The Governor and legislators appealed to the Supreme Court.","question":"Do the use of multi-member districts and of a registered-voters apportionment base violate the Equal Protection Clause, so that the legislature's interim senate plan, together with the existing house apportionment, must be rejected?","holding":"The interim senate plan together with the existing house apportionment has not been shown to violate federal standards. The Equal Protection Clause does not require single-member districts, and a registered-voters base is acceptable only because on this record it produced a distribution of legislators not substantially different from one based on a permissible population base.","reasoning":"The Court first held that, once the District Court allowed the legislature to act, it should have let it review the whole apportionment scheme, not just the senate, but because the 1966 election was imminent it treated the senate plan and house plan together as one interim proposal. The Equal Protection Clause does not require single-member districts, and multi-member districts are invalid only on a showing, from evidence rather than conjecture, that they \"minimize or cancel out the voting strength\" of racial or political elements; no such showing was made. Reynolds requires that both houses be apportioned \"substantially on a population basis,\" but it does not require total census population as the measure, and states need not count aliens, transients or similar groups. A registered-voters base depends on political activity and chance factors, so it is acceptable only if it approximates a permissible population base. Given Hawaii's large military and tourist populations concentrated on Oahu, the District Court's finding that registered voters approximated state citizen population was not shown to be erroneous. The Court cautioned that this holding was limited to interim use.","separate":"Justice Harlan concurred in the result: he still believed Reynolds v. Sims wrong but felt bound by it, and he disagreed with the Court's rationale in Part III, arguing that a registered-voters base is permissible on any rational basis consistent with Reynolds, whether or not it approximates another population measure. Justice Stewart concurred in the judgment, adhering to his view that the Equal Protection Clause requires only a rational plan that does not permit systematic frustration of majority will, and noting there is room for at least as much flexibility as the Court allowed Hawaii.","disposition":"Vacated and remanded. The District Court was directed to adopt the senate plan and existing house apportionment as an interim apportionment for the 1966 election and to retain jurisdiction. Justice Brennan delivered the opinion of the Court; Justice Fortas took no part, and the opinion text does not state a vote count.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep384/usrep384073/usrep384073.pdf"},"1966/615":{"facts":"Berger was convicted of conspiracy to bribe the Chairman of the New York State Liquor Authority. A bar owner, Pansini, had complained to the district attorney that Authority agents raided his bar after he refused to pay a bribe for a license. Using a recording device, Pansini interviewed an Authority employee and attorney Neyer, and on that evidence a state justice issued an eavesdrop order under the state statute, section 813-a, permitting a recording device in Neyer's office for 60 days. Leads from that eavesdrop produced a second 60-day order for the office of Harry Steinman. About two weeks of eavesdropping uncovered a conspiracy over liquor licenses in which Berger was a go-between, and the recordings were played at his trial.","procedure":"The state trial court upheld the statute, the Appellate Division affirmed without opinion, and the New York Court of Appeals affirmed by a divided vote. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Is New York's permissive eavesdropping statute, section 813-a, consistent with the Fourth and Fourteenth Amendments, given that it authorizes electronic eavesdropping by court order?","holding":"The statute is invalid on its face because its language is \"too broad in its sweep\", permitting a trespassory intrusion into a constitutionally protected area. Conversation is protected by the Fourth Amendment, and electronic eavesdropping is a search that must meet the Amendment's probable cause and particularity requirements.","reasoning":"The Court reviewed the history of eavesdropping and its own cases, stating that conversation is within the Fourth Amendment's protections and that using electronic devices to capture it is a \"search.\" Although the statute provides for a neutral judge, the Court found it deficient in several ways. It does not require belief that a particular offense has been or is being committed, or a particular description of the conversations to be seized, giving the officer \"a roving commission\". A two-month order is the equivalent of a series of searches on one showing of probable cause, and extensions need only be \"in the public interest\" rather than based on present probable cause. It sets no termination date once the conversation sought is obtained, requires no notice or showing of exigent circumstances, and requires no return on the warrant. By contrast, the Court noted that the order upheld in Osborn was issued with precise and discriminate safeguards. The Court said it could not forgive the requirements of the Fourth Amendment in the name of law enforcement, and that proper procedures might still be devised.","separate":"Justice Douglas concurred, joining the opinion because it in effect overrules Olmstead, but maintaining that electronic surveillance is always an unconstitutional search for \"mere evidence\". Justice Stewart concurred in the result: he thought the statute constitutional but would reverse because the affidavits for the Steinman order did not show probable cause adequate for so broad an intrusion. Justice Black dissented, arguing the Fourth Amendment does not reach eavesdropping on conversations, that the Court wrongly rewrote it to protect \"privacy,\" and that the statute contains many safeguards. Justice Harlan dissented, objecting to facial review of the statute, to ignoring the state courts' construction of it, and to the Court's view of the order, which he found supported by probable cause. Justice White dissented, arguing that the Steinman affidavits met Fourth Amendment requirements, that the Court created new warrant requirements, and that it undervalued law enforcement needs.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Clark delivered the opinion of the Court; the opinion text does not state a vote count. Justices Black, Harlan and White dissented, and Justices Douglas and Stewart wrote separately concurring.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep388/usrep388041/usrep388041.pdf"},"1968/65":{"facts":"Dr. Timothy Leary left New York by car for Mexico with his daughter, son and two others. After apparently being denied entry at the Mexican customs station in Laredo, Texas, they drove back across the International Bridge. A customs inspector found marihuana seeds, then small amounts of marihuana in the car and on the daughter's person. Leary testified that he had acquired the marihuana in New York, had driven with it to Laredo and across the bridge and back, and did not know where it had been grown. He admitted he had not obtained an order form or paid the transfer tax under the Marihuana Tax Act.","procedure":"Leary was tried in the federal District Court for the Southern District of Texas on three counts; the smuggling count was dismissed, and the jury convicted him of transporting illegally imported marihuana with knowledge of its importation (21 U.S.C. § 176a) and of transporting marihuana acquired without payment of the transfer tax (26 U.S.C. § 4744(a)(2)). The Fifth Circuit affirmed, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari on the self-incrimination and due process questions.","question":"(1) Did Leary's conviction for failing to comply with the Marihuana Tax Act's transfer tax provisions violate his Fifth Amendment privilege against self-incrimination? (2) Did applying the § 176a presumption, under which possession of marihuana is sufficient evidence that it was illegally imported and that the possessor knew of the illegal importation, deny him due process?","holding":"(1) A timely and proper assertion of the privilege against self-incrimination was a complete defense to a prosecution under § 4744(a)(2), because compliance with the transfer tax provisions would have exposed a nonregistrant to a \"real and appreciable\" risk of incrimination. (2) The part of the § 176a presumption that lets the jury infer knowledge of illegal importation from possession is unconstitutional, because it cannot be said with substantial assurance that a possessor is more likely than not to know the marihuana was imported.","reasoning":"Following Marchetti, Grosso and Haynes, the Court found that a nonregistrant who sought an order form would have identified himself as a member of a selective group inherently suspect of criminal activities, and that the information would be available to state and local prosecutors in States where possession was a crime. The Court rejected the Government's argument that regulations and administrative practice barred nonregistrants from obtaining forms, concluding from the statute's text and legislative history that Congress meant such transfers to be taxed, not forbidden, and that the regulations were contrary to the statute. It declined to impose use restrictions on the information because the Act aimed at bringing violations to light. It held the claim of privilege timely and not waived by Leary's testifying. On the presumption, the Court applied the Tot, Gainey and Romano rational-connection test, which requires that the presumed fact be \"more likely than not to flow from the proved fact\". It left the importation inference undecided and examined the knowledge inference empirically. It considered five ways a possessor might know the source of his marihuana and found no basis to say that even a majority had learned it, particularly since a not inconsiderable share of domestic marihuana appeared to be home-grown.","separate":"Chief Justice Warren joined Part II and, considering himself bound by Marchetti, Grosso and Haynes, concurred in the result as to Part I. Justice Stewart joined Part II and, while doubting that the privilege reaches beyond testimony in judicial proceedings, joined Part I because precedent left no escape from that conclusion. Justice Black concurred in the result on both counts; he joined the reversal on Count 3 for the reasons in Part I, and invalidated the presumption not as irrational or arbitrary but because Congress cannot tell a jury what evidence suffices to convict, which he viewed as a denial of due process and the right to a full trial.","disposition":"Reversed outright as to the conviction under 26 U.S.C. § 4744(a)(2) (Count 3); reversed and remanded to the Court of Appeals as to the § 176a conviction (Count 2). Justice Harlan delivered the opinion of the Court; the text does not state a vote count, and shows Warren, Stewart and Black as noted above.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep395/usrep395006/usrep395006.pdf"},"1967/90":{"facts":"After the Court held in Phillips Petroleum Co. v. Wisconsin that independent natural gas producers are natural gas companies under the Natural Gas Act, the Federal Power Commission found that setting rates producer by producer on a cost-of-service basis was unworkable because of the huge number of producers and filings. In 1960 it began area proceedings to set maximum producer rates for each major producing area, starting with the Permian Basin in Texas and New Mexico. In 1965 it fixed two area maximum prices, a higher one for gas-well gas dedicated to interstate commerce after January 1, 1961, and a lower one for other gas, with a 12% return, a minimum price, quality and Btu adjustments, a moratorium on filing rate increases, a ban on indefinite escalation clauses, exemptions for small producers, and refund requirements.","procedure":"The Tenth Circuit upheld the Commission's authority to impose area rates, the moratorium, the two-price system and the small-producer exemptions, but set aside the orders for failing to satisfy Hope Natural Gas. It found inadequate findings on the effects of the quality adjustments and on aggregate revenue, imprecise relief provisions, and improper refund provisions, and remanded. The Supreme Court granted certiorari and consolidated the many cases.","question":"Did the Commission have authority under the Constitution and the Natural Gas Act to regulate producers through area maximum rates and ancillary requirements, and was the Permian Basin rate structure, including its refund orders, just and reasonable and adequately supported?","holding":"The Commission may regulate producers by area maximum rates and supplementary requirements, and its Permian Basin rate structure and refund orders were within its authority; the producers did not carry their burden of showing the rates unjust or unreasonable in result. Courts review whether the Commission abused its authority, whether each essential element has substantial evidence, and whether the order reasonably protects investors and the public interest.","reasoning":"The Court said review is narrow: a presumption of validity attaches to the Commission's exercise of expertise, and challengers undertake \"the heavy burden of making a convincing showing\" that a rate is unjust and unreasonable in its consequences. The Constitution permits maximum prices and group rate-setting if rates balance investor and consumer interests, and the Commission's provisions for special relief and abandonment were not inadequate. The Act's text and purposes support a generous construction allowing area regulation, a brief moratorium on rate filings, limits on indefinite escalation clauses, separate treatment of small producers, and designating the Permian Basin area without hearings on its boundaries. The Court sustained the two-price structure as a permissible use of price as an incentive, the use of national and area cost data, the use of flowing gas-well gas costs for old gas, the rate base method, and the 12% return, and held that treating quality adjustments as a risk was permissible and supported by adequate findings. The producers failed to show that aggregate revenue was inadequate, and the Commission's refund orders were not limited to periods of group revenue surplus. Because eight years had passed, the Court decided the remaining issues itself rather than remanding for further proceedings.","separate":"Justice Douglas dissented, arguing that the Court's decision cannot be reconciled with Hope Natural Gas because the Commission found only averages without determining they were typical and representative, so the effect on producers cannot be known. He objected that treating quality adjustments as a \"risk\" instead of a cost evaded findings, that conditions for individual relief were undefined, and that the interests of producing States such as Texas and New Mexico should have been weighed. The text provided ends at page 845, and no other separate opinions appear in it.","disposition":"Judgments of the Court of Appeals affirmed in part and reversed in part, the Commission's orders sustained, and the cases remanded for further proceedings consistent with the opinion; the motions to adduce additional evidence were denied. Justice Harlan delivered the opinion of the Court; Justice Marshall took no part, and the opinion text does not state a vote count.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep390/usrep390747/usrep390747.pdf"},"1968/370":{"facts":"Zwickler was convicted under a New York statute for distributing anonymous handbills criticizing a Congressman during the 1964 election. The conviction was reversed on state-law grounds without reaching the constitutional questions. In 1966 he sued in federal court for a declaration that the statute was unconstitutional, alleging he intended to distribute the 1964 handbill and similar anonymous leaflets in the 1966 election and later ones. By the time of the earlier Supreme Court argument the Congressman had left the House for the New York Supreme Court.","procedure":"A three-judge District Court first abstained, and the Supreme Court held that abstention was error and remanded, requiring Zwickler to establish the elements governing a declaratory judgment. On remand, without taking testimony, the District Court held those elements existed when the suit began, found the Congressman's departure immaterial, and declared the statute invalid. The Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction.","question":"Did Zwickler present a controversy of sufficient immediacy and reality, at the time of the hearing on remand, to support a declaratory judgment that the New York anonymous-handbill statute is unconstitutional?","holding":"No. The proper time to test for an actual controversy was the hearing on remand, not the filing of the suit, and because it was most unlikely the Congressman would again be a candidate, the prospect of a further prosecution was conjectural. Federal courts may not rule on constitutionality absent a specific live grievance, so the complaint should have been dismissed.","reasoning":"The Court said the District Court asked the wrong question by looking to when the action was initiated rather than to the hearing on remand. It recalled that federal courts do not render advisory opinions and that the Declaratory Judgment Act is limited to cases of actual controversy, which requires a substantial controversy between adverse parties of \"sufficient immediacy and reality\". The complaint focused on the Congressman's re-election and the 1964 handbill and similar leaflets, and the statute reaches only handbills directly about election campaigns. Since a new campaign involving the Congressman was neither real nor immediate, Zwickler's brief assertion that he could run again was no substitute for evidence. A general interest in a broad adjudication, in his own and others' interest, did not supply a specific live grievance. The Court therefore expressed no view on the statute's constitutionality.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded with direction to dismiss the complaint. Justice Brennan delivered the opinion of the Court; the text shows no separate opinions and does not state a vote count.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep394/usrep394103/usrep394103.pdf"},"1968/645":{"facts":"After a rape in Meridian, Mississippi, police without warrants took at least 24 Black youths to headquarters for brief questioning and fingerprinting, and the only leads were latent prints from a window. Petitioner, a 14-year-old, was fingerprinted and released on December 3. On December 12 police, conceded to lack a warrant or probable cause, drove him 90 miles to Jackson and held him overnight. He was returned to Meridian and fingerprinted again on December 14, and those prints were sent to the FBI and matched the window prints.","procedure":"Petitioner was tried for rape in a Mississippi circuit court, and the fingerprint evidence was admitted over his objection that it was the product of unlawful detention. He was convicted and sentenced to life imprisonment, and the Mississippi Supreme Court affirmed. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Must fingerprints obtained during an illegal detention, without probable cause or a warrant, be excluded from evidence at trial under the Fourth and Fourteenth Amendments?","holding":"Yes. Fingerprint evidence is not exempt from the exclusionary rule, and the Fourth Amendment applies to investigatory detentions and to detentions solely to obtain fingerprints. Because the detentions here met no Fourth Amendment requirements, the fingerprints should have been excluded.","reasoning":"The Court rejected the state court's view that fingerprints' trustworthiness exempts them, since the rule excludes illegally seized evidence however reliable and fingerprints share the common characteristic of being evidence yielded during illegal detention. The December 14 prints were taken during a detention the State conceded was without probable cause, and the Court found the December 3 prints also not validly obtained. It rejected the argument that the Fourth Amendment does not reach the investigatory stage, stressing that it was meant to prevent wholesale intrusions on personal security however labeled, citing Terry v. Ohio. The Court observed that fingerprinting may be a less serious intrusion, so narrowly circumscribed procedures might possibly satisfy the Amendment, but it left that question open. No such procedures were used here: no judicial authorization, two fingerprinting sessions, and interrogation as well.","separate":"Justice Harlan concurred, joining the opinion except for its dictum that the requirement of advance judicial authorization seemingly admits of no exception for fingerprinting; he would leave that question open. Justice Black dissented, arguing that the Court was expanding the exclusionary rule and the Fourth Amendment beyond their intended scope and that the record showed the youths cooperated. Justice Stewart dissented, agreeing the detention lacked probable cause but arguing that fingerprints, being an unchanging characteristic that can be reproduced, are not tainted evidence and that reversal was a useless gesture.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Brennan delivered the opinion of the Court; Justice Fortas took no part. The text does not state a vote count.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep394/usrep394721/usrep394721.pdf"},"1959/48":{"facts":"The United States sued a taxpayer to collect a 1946 income tax deficiency and statutory interest. The taxpayer had signed a Treasury form waiving the restrictions on assessment and collection of the deficiency, but the Commissioner had never sent a 90-day notice of deficiency. The taxpayer defended on the ground that §272(a)(1) of the 1939 Internal Revenue Code bars assessment or suit until such a notice has been mailed and the 90-day period has run.","procedure":"The District Court held the waiver ineffective because no 90-day letter had issued, so §272(a) barred the action, and the Court of Appeals (Ninth Circuit) affirmed. The Supreme Court granted certiorari because the First and Sixth Circuits had decided the question the other way.","question":"Does §272(d) of the 1939 Internal Revenue Code allow a taxpayer to waive the restrictions on assessment and collection of a deficiency before the Commissioner has issued a 90-day notice of deficiency, so that the Government may sue without one?","holding":"Yes. A waiver filed under §272(d) is a fully effective instrument even when executed before a notice of deficiency issues, so the absence of a 90-day letter does not bar collection.","reasoning":"The Court began with the text: §272(d) gives the right to waive \"at any time\" and covers all the restrictions in §272(a), including those that apply before a notice is mailed. It rejected the argument that a deficiency is not determined until notice is sent, calling that a fine-spun refinement. The legislative history of the 1926 waiver provision showed its purpose was to let a taxpayer pay and stop interest running, and the taxpayer's reading would leave him unable to stop interest until a formal notice issued. The Court found the 1938 House subcommittee report ambiguous and treated Congress's failure to adopt its proposed amendment as no basis for inference. It also gave little weight to the 1954 Code's express authorization of pre-notice waivers and its reports, since they reflect the views of a later Congress.","separate":"Justice Douglas, joined by Justice Stewart, dissented in a short opinion. He said Mutual Lumber Co. v. Poe states the correct rule, that Congress had not changed it, and that he would affirm.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded. Justice Harlan delivered the opinion of the Court; Justices Douglas and Stewart dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep361/usrep361304/usrep361304.pdf"},"1960/174":{"facts":"Two Iowa prisoners, Smith and Marshall, each sent a petition for a writ of habeas corpus, with a motion to proceed in forma pauperis and an affidavit of poverty, to the clerk of the Lee County district court. Smith challenged the warrant under which his parole was revoked; Marshall attacked the information to which he had pleaded guilty and claimed his plea was coerced. The clerk refused to docket either petition without the $4 statutory filing fee, and a $3 fee also applied to appeals. Iowa conceded that both men were indigent and that the fee effectively denied them the writ.","procedure":"The Iowa Supreme Court denied each prisoner leave to appeal in forma pauperis (Smith's without opinion). The Supreme Court treated Smith's appeal as a petition for certiorari and granted certiorari in both cases, limited to the fee question.","question":"Does a State deny indigent prisoners the equal protection of the laws by requiring a filing fee before it will docket an application for habeas corpus, or an appeal in such a proceeding?","holding":"Yes. Under the Fourteenth Amendment, \"to interpose any financial consideration between an indigent prisoner of the State and his exercise of a state right to sue for his liberty is to deny that prisoner the equal protection of the laws.\"","reasoning":"The Court relied on Griffin v. Illinois and Burns v. Ohio, which barred financial barriers to appeals by indigent criminal defendants. Iowa argued that habeas corpus is a collateral, civil and statutory proceeding, but the Court declined to let the result turn on those labels and stressed the writ's history as the great safeguard of personal liberty. It said that although the fee was small, to a person who cannot get the money it might as well be $400, and that Iowa had made the writ available only to those who can pay. The Court also rejected the argument that indigents could go to federal court, noting that the state remedy may reach questions not raisable in federal habeas. It limited its holding to indigent convicted prisoners and did not decide whether a State must offer post-conviction relief at all.","disposition":"Judgments of the Iowa Supreme Court vacated and the causes remanded. Justice Clark delivered the opinion of the Court; the text shows no separate opinions or recorded dissents.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep365/usrep365708/usrep365708.pdf"},"1960/28":{"facts":"Konigsberg passed the California bar examination and applied for certification by the Committee of Bar Examiners. California law required an applicant to prove good moral character and forbade certification of anyone who advocates violent overthrow of government. In Committee hearings he repeatedly refused to answer questions about Communist Party membership, not on self-incrimination grounds but because he said the questions exceeded the Committee's authority and infringed free thought, association and expression. He affirmed that he did not believe in violent overthrow.","procedure":"After his first rejection, the Supreme Court (in Konigsberg v. State Bar, 353 U. S. 252) held that decision unsupported by the evidence and remanded. On remand the Committee again refused certification, this time because his refusals to answer had obstructed a full investigation of his qualifications, and the California Supreme Court, by a divided vote, refused review and denied admission. The Court granted certiorari again.","question":"May a State deny admission to the bar because an applicant refuses to answer questions about Communist Party membership, consistently with the Court's earlier Konigsberg decision, the Fourteenth Amendment's bar on arbitrary state action, and the First and Fourteenth Amendment rights of speech and association?","holding":"Yes. The denial did not violate the Fourteenth Amendment: a State may deny admission \"so long as he refuses to provide unprivileged answers to questions having a substantial relevance to his qualifications,\" and the applicant had no constitutional privilege to refuse the Party-membership questions.","reasoning":"First, the earlier decision decided only that the evidence could not support a finding of bad character; it left open whether refusal to answer could itself be a ground for exclusion, and the remand was not inconsistent with it. Second, the Court treated the Committee's rule like a procedural rule of an investigation, comparable to sanctions for refusing to answer in civil litigation; applying it was neither arbitrary nor discriminatory, the questions were relevant, and Konigsberg had been clearly warned. Third, the Court rejected the idea that speech and association are absolutes and weighed the interests: the State's interest in the fitness of lawyers outweighed the minimal effect of compelled disclosure in private hearings subject to judicial review. Speiser v. Randall was distinguished because no intent to penalize political beliefs was shown and California had not clearly put the burden of proof on advocacy on the applicant.","separate":"Justice Black, joined by the Chief Justice and Justice Douglas, dissented. He argued that no authoritative California rule made refusal to answer a ground for exclusion, that the questions directly abridged speech and association contrary to the First Amendment's plain command, that the Court's balancing test was unsound and in any event misapplied, and that the case was controlled by Speiser. Justice Brennan, joined by the Chief Justice, dissented separately, reasoning that under Speiser the Committee, having produced no evidence of unlawful advocacy, could not bar him for refusing to answer.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Harlan delivered the opinion of the Court; Justices Black, Douglas and Brennan and the Chief Justice dissented, as the dissenting opinions show.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep366/usrep366036/usrep366036.pdf"},"1961/643":{"facts":"Black residents of Jackson, Mississippi, sued in federal district court on behalf of themselves and others similarly situated. They sought injunctions to enforce their constitutional right to nonsegregated service in interstate and intrastate transportation, alleging that the right had been denied under state statutes, municipal ordinances, and state custom and usage.","procedure":"A three-judge District Court, Circuit Judge Rives dissenting, abstained pending construction of the challenged laws by the state courts. The plaintiffs appealed directly to the Supreme Court under 28 U. S. C. § 1253, and the Court had earlier denied a motion to stay a number of pending criminal prosecutions.","question":"Did the plaintiffs have standing to seek relief, and was a three-judge court required (so that direct appeal lay) where state-enforced segregation of transportation facilities had already been held unconstitutional in prior decisions?","holding":"The plaintiffs lack standing to enjoin breach-of-peace prosecutions but, as passengers, may enforce their right to nonsegregated treatment. Because the unconstitutionality of such segregation is \"foreclosed as a litigable issue,\" no three-judge court was required, so direct appeal was improper, and the Court vacated and remanded.","reasoning":"The Court held the plaintiffs could not enjoin prosecutions under the breach-of-peace statutes because they alleged no prosecution or threat of prosecution and could not represent a class to which they did not belong. As passengers using segregated facilities, however, they were aggrieved parties. It cited Morgan v. Virginia, Gayle v. Browder and Boynton v. Virginia as having settled that no State may require segregation of transportation, so the constitutional claim was frivolous. Section 2281 requires a three-judge court only when the constitutional question is substantial; the policy behind it does not apply where the issue is \"essentially fictitious.\" Since a three-judge court was not required, the case could not come by direct appeal under § 1253, but the Court could determine the lower court's authority and make a corrective order.","disposition":"Judgment vacated and case remanded to the District Court for expeditious disposition of the claims to nonsegregated service. Per curiam; the text shows no separate opinions.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep369/usrep369031/usrep369031.pdf"},"1961/8_orig":{"facts":"Arizona filed an original action in 1952 against California and seven of its public agencies over how much Colorado River water each State may use; Nevada, New Mexico, Utah and the United States were later added. The dispute turned on the Boulder Canyon Project Act of 1928, which authorized Hoover Dam and related works, limited California to 4,400,000 acre-feet of the first 7,500,000 acre-feet apportioned to the Lower Basin by the Colorado River Compact, and authorized a tri-state compact that was never made. The Secretary of the Interior then contracted with water users for 5,362,000 acre-feet in California, 2,800,000 in Arizona and 300,000 in Nevada. The United States also claimed water for Indian reservations and other federal lands.","procedure":"The Court appointed a Special Master, who held a lengthy trial and filed a report and recommended decree. The parties took exceptions, and the case came to the Court on those exceptions after two arguments.","question":"Does the Project Act apportion the Lower Basin's mainstream Colorado River water among Arizona, California and Nevada and give the Secretary of the Interior power, through his contracts, to allocate it, or do equitable apportionment, the Compact or state prior-appropriation law control; and what water rights do the Indian reservations and other federal lands have?","holding":"Congress created its own complete scheme for apportioning the Lower Basin's mainstream water (4,400,000 acre-feet to California, 2,800,000 to Arizona, 300,000 to Nevada, with any surplus split between Arizona and California), leaving tributaries to each State, and gave the Secretary power to carry it out by contracts without being bound by state law. The Court also held that the United States reserved water for the Indian reservations, measured by irrigable acreage, and that in shortage the Secretary may choose among recognized methods or devise \"reasonable methods of his own.\"","reasoning":"The Court held that equitable apportionment does not apply where Congress has made its own apportionment, and that the Compact matters only for terms the Act incorporates and for the division between basins. From the negotiations, the Denver proposals and the Senate debates, it concluded the Act dealt only with mainstream water. Section 5 provides that \"No person shall have or be entitled to have the use for any purpose of the water stored as aforesaid except by contract,\" and the Court read this, with the deletion of language preserving prior appropriators, as giving the Secretary the power to choose contractors and terms. Sections 14 and 18 and section 8 of the Reclamation Act did not bind him to state law, following Ivanhoe. In shortage the Secretary is not required to prorate and may use any reasonable method consistent with the Act, subject to later review. On federal claims, following Winters v. United States, the creation of the reservations reserved water as well as land, and those rights are \"present perfected rights\" entitled to priority, measured by practicably irrigable acreage.","separate":"Justice Harlan, joined by Justices Douglas and Stewart, dissented in part. He would hold that Congress intended equitable apportionment and state law to govern, that section 5 was mainly a revenue and Compact-enforcement device, and that the Secretary was given no standards for shortages (raising serious delegation doubts). He said he and Justice Stewart, but not Justice Douglas, joined the rest of the Court's opinion. Justice Douglas dissented separately, arguing that section 4(a) and the Compact refer to the whole Colorado River System including tributaries, that state law governs priorities, and that the Court gave the Secretary power Congress never granted.","disposition":"Decided on exceptions to the Special Master's report; the Court allowed the parties to submit a form of decree by September 16, 1963, failing which it would enter a decree. Justice Black delivered the opinion of the Court; the Chief Justice took no part; Justices Harlan, Douglas and Stewart dissented in part as stated. No vote count is stated.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep373/usrep373546/usrep373546.pdf"},"1961/32":{"facts":"Hamilton was indicted in Alabama on a count of breaking and entering a dwelling at night with intent to ravish, a capital offense. He had been first indicted for burglary and had appointed counsel at that arraignment. A later indictment on the same incident was returned, and at the re-arraignment no lawyer appeared for him. He pleaded not guilty, was convicted and was sentenced to death.","procedure":"On direct appeal the Alabama Supreme Court did not reach the denial-of-counsel claim because it would require impeaching the trial minute entries, which may not be done on appeal. The U.S. Supreme Court denied certiorari at that stage. Hamilton then sought coram nobis relief, and the Alabama Supreme Court denied it because he had not shown he was disadvantaged by the absence of counsel. The U.S. Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does the absence of counsel at an arraignment on a capital charge in Alabama, without any showing of actual prejudice, violate the Due Process Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment?","holding":"Yes. Because arraignment is a critical stage under Alabama law, a defendant arraigned on a capital charge without counsel is denied due process, and the Court does not inquire whether prejudice resulted.","reasoning":"Justice Douglas explained that under Alabama law arraignment is \"a critical stage in a criminal proceeding\": the insanity defense must be pleaded then or the chance is lost, and pleas in abatement and motions to quash for improper grand jury composition must also be made then. Available defenses can be irretrievably lost if not asserted there. He relied on Powell v. Alabama for the need for counsel at every step in a capital case. Prior cases hold that when one pleads to a capital charge without counsel the Court does not stop to determine whether prejudice resulted. Here \"the degree of prejudice can never be known,\" since only counsel could have enabled the accused to know all his defenses and plead intelligently.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Douglas delivered the opinion of the Court; the text shown does not state a vote count or any separate opinion.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep368/usrep368052/usrep368052.pdf"},"1964/256":{"facts":"Estes, whose case had received massive national publicity, was indicted by a Texas grand jury for swindling, and the trial was moved by change of venue to Tyler. A two-day pretrial hearing on his motion to bar cameras was carried live on television and radio with at least 12 cameramen, cables and microphones in the courtroom, and the hearing was highly publicized. At the trial a month later cameras were confined to a booth at the rear; live telecasting was largely barred except for the State's opening and closing arguments and the verdict, while film clips were shown on news programs. Four of the jurors selected had seen or heard some of the earlier broadcasts.","procedure":"The trial court denied Estes's motion to prevent telecasting and he was convicted. The Texas Court of Criminal Appeals affirmed, rejecting his claim that televising the trial denied him due process. The U.S. Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did the televising and broadcasting of a notorious criminal trial over the defendant's objection deny him due process of law under the Fourteenth Amendment, even without a showing of specific prejudice?","holding":"Yes. Televising the courtroom proceedings of a criminal trial of widespread public interest, over the defendant's objection, is inherently invalid under the Due Process Clause, and prejudice need not be shown.","reasoning":"Justice Clark first held that the pretrial hearing was relevant because its publicity stressed the notorious character of the case. He rejected the First Amendment and public-trial arguments, since the press, including television, may attend and report but has no right to broadcast from the courtroom. Citing Murchison, Tumey, Rideau and Turner, he said that where a procedure carries a probability of prejudice it is inherently lacking in due process without proof of actual prejudice. He listed ways television can cause unfairness: it affects jurors, impairs witnesses' testimony and frustrates the rule against witnesses, burdens and distracts the judge, and pressures the defendant and intrudes on counsel. He noted these effects are not merely hypothetical, as federal rules and nearly all States bar television, and \"Trial by television is, therefore, foreign to our system.\" The facts here, including the televised pretrial hearing and the trial judge's shifting orders, showed the need to apply the Rideau rule, and the Court must decide on facts as they are today.","separate":"Chief Justice Warren, joined by Justices Douglas and Goldberg, concurred, adding that televised criminal trials violate the Sixth and Fourteenth Amendments because they divert the trial from its purpose, mislead the public about trials, and single out some defendants, and that the record also showed actual prejudice. Justice Harlan concurred subject to reservations, limiting his view to a notorious trial like this one and leaving other cases open. Justice Stewart, joined by Justices Black, Brennan and White, dissented, saying televising is unwise but the record shows no denial of fair trial and he could not find a per se constitutional ban; he also thought the pretrial hearing was not properly before the Court. Justice White, joined by Justice Brennan, dissented separately as a ban was premature given limited experience. Justice Brennan wrote that only four of the five Justices voting to reverse supported a ban on televising criminal trials under all circumstances.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Clark delivered the opinion of the Court, in which Chief Justice Warren and Justices Douglas and Goldberg joined and Justice Harlan joined with reservations; four Justices dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep381/usrep381532/usrep381532.pdf"},"1961/33":{"facts":"Charles Dowd Box Co. employed workers represented by a local of the United Steelworkers. After negotiations in 1957, representatives of the company and union signed a Stipulation continuing most provisions of the old agreement with wage increases, and the terms were put in a draft agreement. The company first announced it would apply the changes but then said it would return to the earlier rates, claiming its bargaining representatives lacked authority and no contract had been concluded. Local union officers and a staff representative sued in Massachusetts Superior Court for a declaration that a binding agreement existed, an injunction, an accounting and damages.","procedure":"The company argued that § 301(a) of the Labor Management Relations Act deprived the state court of jurisdiction. The trial court rejected this, found the agreement valid and binding, and entered a money judgment. The Massachusetts Supreme Judicial Court affirmed, holding § 301(a) did not make federal jurisdiction exclusive. The U.S. Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does § 301(a) of the Labor Management Relations Act, which authorizes suits on collective bargaining contracts in federal district courts, divest state courts of jurisdiction over such suits?","holding":"No. Section 301(a) does not make federal jurisdiction exclusive, and state courts retain concurrent jurisdiction over suits for violation of contracts between employers and labor organizations.","reasoning":"Justice Stewart noted that the statute says such suits may be brought in federal court, not that they must be, and nothing in it suggests exclusivity. He rejected the argument that Textile Workers v. Lincoln Mills, which called for a body of federal common law, requires exclusive federal jurisdiction, finding nothing showing Congress adopted that policy. Under Claflin v. Houseman, state courts may exercise concurrent jurisdiction over federal claims unless excluded by express provision or by incompatibility arising from the nature of the case. The legislative history of § 301 and its predecessor in the Case bill shows the purpose of federal jurisdiction was \"not to displace, but to supplement\" state jurisdiction, in light of weak state remedies against unincorporated unions, and Senator Ferguson said state jurisdiction would not be taken away. Unlike the NLRB scheme in Garner, Congress chose to leave enforcement of agreements \"to the usual processes of the law,\" so possible diversity among courts is the ordinary consequence of concurrent jurisdiction.","separate":"Justice Black concurred in the result; the text gives no reasons.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Stewart delivered the opinion of the Court; the text shown does not state a vote count, and Justice Black concurred in the result.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep368/usrep368502/usrep368502.pdf"}});